window.TERMS=[{"id":0,"term":"Marketing","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":1,"definition":"The activity of creating, communicating and delivering value. Marketing begins with value, and advertising is only one of the many things marketing does.","explanation":"The starting point is the value offered to the customer, not the advertisement used to promote it. Research, segmentation, product development, pricing, distribution, branding, content, service and customer relationships are all marketing activities. A campaign cannot compensate for an offer that does not create value for the people it is meant to serve.","example":"For instance, a retailer choosing which products to offer, how to price them, where to sell them and how to serve customers is making marketing decisions before creating an advertisement. This is why an advertising campaign alone is not a complete marketing strategy.","full":"The activity of creating, communicating and delivering value. Marketing begins with value, and advertising is only one of the many things marketing does.\n\nThe starting point is the value offered to the customer, not the advertisement used to promote it. Research, segmentation, product development, pricing, distribution, branding, content, service and customer relationships are all marketing activities. A campaign cannot compensate for an offer that does not create value for the people it is meant to serve.\n\nFor instance, a retailer choosing which products to offer, how to price them, where to sell them and how to serve customers is making marketing decisions before creating an advertisement. This is why an advertising campaign alone is not a complete marketing strategy.","category":"MARKETING FUNDAMENTALS"},{"id":1,"term":"Advertising","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":1,"definition":"Paid communication. It is one marketing activity among many, and it exists both online and offline.","explanation":"Advertising is a specific communication activity within the wider field of marketing. Paying for a social media ad, a search ad or a billboard is advertising; developing the offer, setting its price and serving customers are also marketing, but are not themselves advertising. The defining feature is the paid communication, not whether the channel is digital.","example":"For instance, an organisation buying a placement in a newspaper and buying a sponsored post on a social platform are both advertising. The channels differ, but the communication is paid in both cases.","full":"Paid communication. It is one marketing activity among many, and it exists both online and offline.\n\nAdvertising is a specific communication activity within the wider field of marketing. Paying for a social media ad, a search ad or a billboard is advertising; developing the offer, setting its price and serving customers are also marketing, but are not themselves advertising. The defining feature is the paid communication, not whether the channel is digital.\n\nFor instance, an organisation buying a placement in a newspaper and buying a sponsored post on a social platform are both advertising. The channels differ, but the communication is paid in both cases.","category":"MARKETING FUNDAMENTALS"},{"id":2,"term":"Digital marketing","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":1,"definition":"Marketing carried out in a digital environment. It applies the same fundamentals as all marketing — product, price, place and promotion — but depends on technology.","explanation":"Digital technology changes how each of the four Ps operates: products may become services, prices can be compared or adjusted rapidly, place includes search results and feeds, and promotion can travel through brands, partners and customers. It also depends on the digital environment through which people discover and interact with offers. Digital marketing is broader than paid digital advertising.","example":"For example, a business can help people discover its products through search, let them purchase through a website and support them through digital services. Those activities form part of digital marketing even if the business purchases no ads.","full":"Marketing carried out in a digital environment. It applies the same fundamentals as all marketing — product, price, place and promotion — but depends on technology.\n\nDigital technology changes how each of the four Ps operates: products may become services, prices can be compared or adjusted rapidly, place includes search results and feeds, and promotion can travel through brands, partners and customers. It also depends on the digital environment through which people discover and interact with offers. Digital marketing is broader than paid digital advertising.\n\nFor example, a business can help people discover its products through search, let them purchase through a website and support them through digital services. Those activities form part of digital marketing even if the business purchases no ads.","category":"MARKETING FUNDAMENTALS"},{"id":3,"term":"Digital advertising","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":2,"definition":"Paid communication placed in digital channels. It is only one part of digital marketing.","explanation":"The lecture identifies paid search, paid social, display and programmatic advertising, and paid influencer partnerships as examples. These are different ways of paying to place promotional messages in digital environments. SEO, organic social media, digital services and customer experience can be part of digital marketing without being paid advertising.","example":"For instance, paying for a listing in search results, a sponsored social post or a labelled paid influencer post is digital advertising. Publishing an unpaid article or offering customer support online is digital marketing, but not paid digital advertising.","full":"Paid communication placed in digital channels. It is only one part of digital marketing.\n\nThe lecture identifies paid search, paid social, display and programmatic advertising, and paid influencer partnerships as examples. These are different ways of paying to place promotional messages in digital environments. SEO, organic social media, digital services and customer experience can be part of digital marketing without being paid advertising.\n\nFor instance, paying for a listing in search results, a sponsored social post or a labelled paid influencer post is digital advertising. Publishing an unpaid article or offering customer support online is digital marketing, but not paid digital advertising.","category":"MARKETING FUNDAMENTALS"},{"id":4,"term":"The four Ps (marketing mix)","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":2,"definition":"Product, price, place and promotion — the marketing mix. It still matters in a digital environment, but each P changes: products become services, prices become dynamic, place becomes search and feed.","explanation":"Product asks what is offered: a music album can become a streaming service. Price asks what customers pay and may become dynamic or easy to compare online. Place asks where an offer can be found, including search engines, feeds, apps and stores. Promotion asks how and by whom it is communicated, including brands, paid partners and customers.","example":"Consider a music service: its product is access to music, its price may be a subscription, its place is an app or website, and its promotion includes messages from the brand, its partners and listeners. The four decisions are connected rather than isolated.","full":"Product, price, place and promotion — the marketing mix. It still matters in a digital environment, but each P changes: products become services, prices become dynamic, place becomes search and feed.\n\nProduct asks what is offered: a music album can become a streaming service. Price asks what customers pay and may become dynamic or easy to compare online. Place asks where an offer can be found, including search engines, feeds, apps and stores. Promotion asks how and by whom it is communicated, including brands, paid partners and customers.\n\nConsider a music service: its product is access to music, its price may be a subscription, its place is an app or website, and its promotion includes messages from the brand, its partners and listeners. The four decisions are connected rather than isolated.","category":"MARKETING FUNDAMENTALS"},{"id":5,"term":"Web 1.0","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":2,"definition":"The read-only web: a library you read. Information was published to an audience that could not easily answer back.","explanation":"Website owners mainly published information and visitors mainly read it. The lecture compares this stage to a library: information was available, but audience participation was limited. This matters because later developments changed users from readers into contributors and made their participation part of the digital environment.","example":"Imagine visiting a company website containing information pages but no easy way to comment, publish your own material or join a discussion. The company speaks and the visitor reads; this is the library comparison used in the lecture.","full":"The read-only web: a library you read. Information was published to an audience that could not easily answer back.\n\nWebsite owners mainly published information and visitors mainly read it. The lecture compares this stage to a library: information was available, but audience participation was limited. This matters because later developments changed users from readers into contributors and made their participation part of the digital environment.\n\nImagine visiting a company website containing information pages but no easy way to comment, publish your own material or join a discussion. The company speaks and the visitor reads; this is the library comparison used in the lecture.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":6,"term":"Web 2.0","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":2,"definition":"Named at a conference in San Francisco in 2004 under the theme “The Web as Platform”. A shift from reading information to creating, sharing and correcting it — blogs, wikis, video sharing, communities, participation.","explanation":"The shift was not just technological; it changed the role of the audience. People could publish blog posts, contribute to wikis, upload videos and participate in communities rather than only read pages. These interactions also produced data and created new possibilities and consequences for marketers.","example":"For instance, on a wiki people can add and correct information; on a video-sharing platform they can publish videos and respond to one another. The user is no longer merely the audience, because the user also helps produce what others see.","full":"Named at a conference in San Francisco in 2004 under the theme “The Web as Platform”. A shift from reading information to creating, sharing and correcting it — blogs, wikis, video sharing, communities, participation.\n\nThe shift was not just technological; it changed the role of the audience. People could publish blog posts, contribute to wikis, upload videos and participate in communities rather than only read pages. These interactions also produced data and created new possibilities and consequences for marketers.\n\nFor instance, on a wiki people can add and correct information; on a video-sharing platform they can publish videos and respond to one another. The user is no longer merely the audience, because the user also helps produce what others see.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":7,"term":"Web 3.0","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":3,"definition":"The predictive web: data, privacy law and prediction. Web 1.0 read, Web 2.0 wrote, Web 3.0 predicts.","explanation":"In the course, this stage is presented through prediction, data and privacy law rather than just publishing or participation. The contrast is useful: Web 1.0 focused on reading, Web 2.0 on writing and contributing, and Web 3.0 on systems drawing predictions from data. That raises questions about how the data is collected and governed.","example":"For marketers this introduces a different question from simply making information available: what can systems predict from collected data, and under what privacy rules should that data be used? The lecture places prediction and regulation alongside one another.","full":"The predictive web: data, privacy law and prediction. Web 1.0 read, Web 2.0 wrote, Web 3.0 predicts.\n\nIn the course, this stage is presented through prediction, data and privacy law rather than just publishing or participation. The contrast is useful: Web 1.0 focused on reading, Web 2.0 on writing and contributing, and Web 3.0 on systems drawing predictions from data. That raises questions about how the data is collected and governed.\n\nFor marketers this introduces a different question from simply making information available: what can systems predict from collected data, and under what privacy rules should that data be used? The lecture places prediction and regulation alongside one another.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":8,"term":"User-generated content","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":3,"definition":"Content created by users rather than by publishers or brands — posts, reviews, comments, wiki entries, uploaded video. The core of Web 2.0.","explanation":"Examples include reviews, comments, uploaded videos and wiki contributions. Instead of brands and publishers having exclusive control over communication, users become contributors whose content other people can find and share. This changes how an organisation is discussed because not every message about it comes from the organisation itself.","example":"A customer review illustrates the change: the organisation may describe its own service, but the customer independently supplies a public account of the experience. Other customers can encounter that account when evaluating the organisation.","full":"Content created by users rather than by publishers or brands — posts, reviews, comments, wiki entries, uploaded video. The core of Web 2.0.\n\nExamples include reviews, comments, uploaded videos and wiki contributions. Instead of brands and publishers having exclusive control over communication, users become contributors whose content other people can find and share. This changes how an organisation is discussed because not every message about it comes from the organisation itself.\n\nA customer review illustrates the change: the organisation may describe its own service, but the customer independently supplies a public account of the experience. Other customers can encounter that account when evaluating the organisation.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":9,"term":"The like button","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":3,"definition":"A small interface element with enormous consequences: it turned approval into a number, made attention countable, and gave marketers real-time feedback.","explanation":"A click of approval became a visible metric that platforms and marketers could count. This makes audience reactions immediately measurable, but it also encourages attention and approval to be treated as numbers. The lecture uses it to show how a very small design choice can change behaviour on a large scale.","example":"For example, a person may evaluate a post partly by its visible number of likes, while a publisher may monitor those likes as immediate feedback. The count is easy to measure, but it does not automatically explain why people responded.","full":"A small interface element with enormous consequences: it turned approval into a number, made attention countable, and gave marketers real-time feedback.\n\nA click of approval became a visible metric that platforms and marketers could count. This makes audience reactions immediately measurable, but it also encourages attention and approval to be treated as numbers. The lecture uses it to show how a very small design choice can change behaviour on a large scale.\n\nFor example, a person may evaluate a post partly by its visible number of likes, while a publisher may monitor those likes as immediate feedback. The count is easy to measure, but it does not automatically explain why people responded.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":10,"term":"Scalable, searchable, replicable","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":3,"definition":"Three qualities of networked content — and their consequences. It can reach a global public, it can be found by anyone at any time, and it can be copied and reused out of context.","explanation":"Scalability means a message can spread far beyond its intended audience; searchability means unexpected people may later find it; replicability means it can be copied and reused in another context. Together, these properties make online publishing powerful but difficult to control. They explain why the consequences of sharing something may continue beyond the original publication.","example":"The important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","full":"Three qualities of networked content — and their consequences. It can reach a global public, it can be found by anyone at any time, and it can be copied and reused out of context.\n\nScalability means a message can spread far beyond its intended audience; searchability means unexpected people may later find it; replicability means it can be copied and reused in another context. Together, these properties make online publishing powerful but difficult to control. They explain why the consequences of sharing something may continue beyond the original publication.\n\nThe important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":11,"term":"GDPR","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":4,"definition":"European data protection regulation that made consent, purpose and data minimisation legal obligations. Regulation is society answering back to a technology it had already adopted.","explanation":"The lecture connects privacy regulation to technological change: after organisations gained new ways to collect and use data, society introduced legal limits and responsibilities. Consent, a defined purpose and collecting only necessary data are central requirements in the course description. Digital marketing therefore cannot treat every possible use of personal data as automatically acceptable.","example":"For example, an organisation collecting information through a digital service must consider what data is actually needed and why it is being collected. Being technologically able to gather more information does not remove those obligations.","full":"European data protection regulation that made consent, purpose and data minimisation legal obligations. Regulation is society answering back to a technology it had already adopted.\n\nThe lecture connects privacy regulation to technological change: after organisations gained new ways to collect and use data, society introduced legal limits and responsibilities. Consent, a defined purpose and collecting only necessary data are central requirements in the course description. Digital marketing therefore cannot treat every possible use of personal data as automatically acceptable.\n\nFor example, an organisation collecting information through a digital service must consider what data is actually needed and why it is being collected. Being technologically able to gather more information does not remove those obligations.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":12,"term":"Trade-off (Postman 1)","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":4,"definition":"All technological change is a trade-off. For every advantage a technology offers, there is a corresponding disadvantage.","explanation":"A tool can solve one problem while creating another. The important question is not whether the technology is simply good or bad, but what advantage it brings and what cost accompanies that advantage. This helps marketers evaluate a new technology beyond its attractive features.","example":"The important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","full":"All technological change is a trade-off. For every advantage a technology offers, there is a corresponding disadvantage.\n\nA tool can solve one problem while creating another. The important question is not whether the technology is simply good or bad, but what advantage it brings and what cost accompanies that advantage. This helps marketers evaluate a new technology beyond its attractive features.\n\nThe important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","category":"ATTENTION AND DISTRIBUTION"},{"id":13,"term":"Uneven distribution (Postman 2)","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":4,"definition":"The advantages and disadvantages of a technology are never distributed evenly. Ask who gains reach or control, whose work becomes easier, and whose skills lose market value.","explanation":"A technology may benefit one group while placing costs on another. For example, a new digital channel can increase the reach of some organisations while reducing the value of skills built around older channels. Postman’s question is who gains, who loses and how these effects differ.","example":"The important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","full":"The advantages and disadvantages of a technology are never distributed evenly. Ask who gains reach or control, whose work becomes easier, and whose skills lose market value.\n\nA technology may benefit one group while placing costs on another. For example, a new digital channel can increase the reach of some organisations while reducing the value of skills built around older channels. Postman’s question is who gains, who loses and how these effects differ.\n\nThe important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change."},{"id":14,"term":"Embedded idea (Postman 3)","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":5,"definition":"Every technology contains a powerful idea or bias. Technologies are not neutral tools: their design favours particular behaviours, values and ways of understanding people.","explanation":"A technology shapes behaviour through its design, even if users treat it as a neutral tool. For example, a system that emphasises visible likes makes approval particularly important and easy to compare. Evaluating a technology therefore includes asking which behaviours it encourages and which values it assumes.","example":"The important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","full":"Every technology contains a powerful idea or bias. Technologies are not neutral tools: their design favours particular behaviours, values and ways of understanding people.\n\nA technology shapes behaviour through its design, even if users treat it as a neutral tool. For example, a system that emphasises visible likes makes approval particularly important and easy to compare. Evaluating a technology therefore includes asking which behaviours it encourages and which values it assumes.\n\nThe important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change."},{"id":15,"term":"Ecological change (Postman 4)","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":5,"definition":"Technological change is ecological, not additive. A new channel does not simply get added — it changes all the existing channels around it.","explanation":"Introducing a new technology changes its surrounding environment rather than merely adding another option. The spread of search, social feeds or assistants changes how existing websites and communication channels are used. A marketer must consider the effects on the entire system, not only the new tool.","example":"The important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","full":"Technological change is ecological, not additive. A new channel does not simply get added — it changes all the existing channels around it.\n\nIntroducing a new technology changes its surrounding environment rather than merely adding another option. The spread of search, social feeds or assistants changes how existing websites and communication channels are used. A marketer must consider the effects on the entire system, not only the new tool.\n\nThe important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change."},{"id":16,"term":"Mythic technology (Postman 5)","lecture":"Lecture 1 · Marketing, technology and change","page":5,"definition":"Technologies tend to become mythic: they come to feel natural and inevitable, so we stop questioning them. Technology can never be the solution in itself — it is a strategy.","explanation":"When a technology becomes ordinary, people can begin to assume it must exist and cannot be questioned. Postman warns against treating the tool itself as the answer. A technology should instead be judged as a possible strategy for a clearly understood problem.","example":"The important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change.","full":"Technologies tend to become mythic: they come to feel natural and inevitable, so we stop questioning them. Technology can never be the solution in itself — it is a strategy.\n\nWhen a technology becomes ordinary, people can begin to assume it must exist and cannot be questioned. Postman warns against treating the tool itself as the answer. A technology should instead be judged as a possible strategy for a clearly understood problem.\n\nThe important point in the lecture is how this changes what people or organisations can do in a digital environment, and which opportunities or consequences follow from that change."},{"id":17,"term":"Web 4.0","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":6,"definition":"The assistant-centric web: from a global library to a global databank, where generative AI and answer engines increasingly stand between people and information.","explanation":"In the lecture, this is a shift from people navigating a library of web pages towards assistants working over a wider databank of information. Generative AI and answer engines can compose responses before a user visits an original source. That changes where people encounter information and what organisations need to consider when making information available.","example":"A person might once compare several webpage results before choosing a source. In the assistant-centric environment described in the lecture, the person may receive a composed answer first, with a source visit becoming optional.","full":"The assistant-centric web: from a global library to a global databank, where generative AI and answer engines increasingly stand between people and information.\n\nIn the lecture, this is a shift from people navigating a library of web pages towards assistants working over a wider databank of information. Generative AI and answer engines can compose responses before a user visits an original source. That changes where people encounter information and what organisations need to consider when making information available.\n\nA person might once compare several webpage results before choosing a source. In the assistant-centric environment described in the lecture, the person may receive a composed answer first, with a source visit becoming optional."},{"id":18,"term":"Answer engine","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":6,"definition":"A system that answers a query directly instead of returning a list of links, changing search, SEO and how people encounter information.","explanation":"Instead of presenting only a list of pages, an answer engine produces a response to a question. This may make obtaining an answer easier, but it also changes whether and how someone visits the pages the information came from. The course connects this development with search, SEO and the newer idea of GEO.","example":"For instance, a person asking which programme offers exchange opportunities may receive a written comparison rather than only links to individual programme pages. The way the answer uses and cites sources becomes important.","full":"A system that answers a query directly instead of returning a list of links, changing search, SEO and how people encounter information.\n\nInstead of presenting only a list of pages, an answer engine produces a response to a question. This may make obtaining an answer easier, but it also changes whether and how someone visits the pages the information came from. The course connects this development with search, SEO and the newer idea of GEO.\n\nFor instance, a person asking which programme offers exchange opportunities may receive a written comparison rather than only links to individual programme pages. The way the answer uses and cites sources becomes important."},{"id":19,"term":"Generative AI","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":6,"definition":"Models that produce text, images or answers from patterns in existing data. In marketing they change both how content is made and how it is found.","explanation":"A generative model can compose content or responses from patterns learned in data. For marketers, this affects content production and the way audiences may receive answers through assistants. The lecture treats this as a change in the digital environment, not just another way of writing copy.","example":"A generative assistant can draft an answer to a user’s question from patterns in its training and other available information. The course highlights that this changes both the creation of material and the point where audiences encounter it.","full":"Models that produce text, images or answers from patterns in existing data. In marketing they change both how content is made and how it is found.\n\nA generative model can compose content or responses from patterns learned in data. For marketers, this affects content production and the way audiences may receive answers through assistants. The lecture treats this as a change in the digital environment, not just another way of writing copy.\n\nA generative assistant can draft an answer to a user’s question from patterns in its training and other available information. The course highlights that this changes both the creation of material and the point where audiences encounter it."},{"id":20,"term":"Algorithmic feed","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":6,"definition":"A feed that is no longer chronological: what people see is chosen by a model estimating what will keep them scrolling. Marketing moves from buying space to earning a prediction.","explanation":"The order of posts is determined by a model rather than simply by publication time. What a user sees depends on predictions about continued attention and engagement. As a result, distribution depends increasingly on platform decisions that the publisher does not directly control.","example":"Two people may open the same platform and see different posts because the feed is selected by a ranking model. The publisher cannot assume that every follower will encounter a post in chronological order.","full":"A feed that is no longer chronological: what people see is chosen by a model estimating what will keep them scrolling. Marketing moves from buying space to earning a prediction.\n\nThe order of posts is determined by a model rather than simply by publication time. What a user sees depends on predictions about continued attention and engagement. As a result, distribution depends increasingly on platform decisions that the publisher does not directly control.\n\nTwo people may open the same platform and see different posts because the feed is selected by a ranking model. The publisher cannot assume that every follower will encounter a post in chronological order."},{"id":21,"term":"Programmatic advertising","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":7,"definition":"Automated, real-time buying and selling of advertising space. It has eroded the gatekeeping role of agencies and made buyers and sellers dependent on data from a handful of major platforms.","explanation":"Software automates the trading of digital ad placements, often extremely quickly. This allows advertising space to be bought and sold on a large scale, but the course also raises concerns about transparency and dependence on platform data. Automation does not guarantee that the resulting exposure is valuable.","example":"For example, software may decide on an advertising placement in a fraction of a second. The lecture asks what the advertiser can actually verify about that placement and whether the measured result represents real audience attention.","full":"Automated, real-time buying and selling of advertising space. It has eroded the gatekeeping role of agencies and made buyers and sellers dependent on data from a handful of major platforms.\n\nSoftware automates the trading of digital ad placements, often extremely quickly. This allows advertising space to be bought and sold on a large scale, but the course also raises concerns about transparency and dependence on platform data. Automation does not guarantee that the resulting exposure is valuable.\n\nFor example, software may decide on an advertising placement in a fraction of a second. The lecture asks what the advertiser can actually verify about that placement and whether the measured result represents real audience attention.","category":"ATTENTION AND DISTRIBUTION"},{"id":22,"term":"Dark pools","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":7,"definition":"Opaque parts of the programmatic marketplace where the advertiser can no longer see how the money is spent or how the advertisement is placed.","explanation":"These refer to opaque or private parts of the advertising marketplace. The central problem is that buyers cannot fully inspect the placement or trading activity behind what they purchase. The lecture connects this lack of transparency to doubts about the real value of digital advertising.","example":"The lecture asks us to consider both the new capability and its consequence for how attention, information or advertising value is distributed online.","full":"Opaque parts of the programmatic marketplace where the advertiser can no longer see how the money is spent or how the advertisement is placed.\n\nThese refer to opaque or private parts of the advertising marketplace. The central problem is that buyers cannot fully inspect the placement or trading activity behind what they purchase. The lecture connects this lack of transparency to doubts about the real value of digital advertising.\n\nThe lecture asks us to consider both the new capability and its consequence for how attention, information or advertising value is distributed online.","category":"ATTENTION AND DISTRIBUTION"},{"id":23,"term":"Subprime attention crisis","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":7,"definition":"Tim Hwang's argument that the value of online attention is systematically overstated: a marketplace of fraud and unverifiable prices, while the attention actually bought is worth less and less.","explanation":"Tim Hwang compares parts of the online advertising market with financial markets in which uncertain products are sold as though their value is reliable. An ad may not be seen, a click may be fraudulent, or a purchase may have happened without the ad. The important distinction is between what advertisers can count and the real attention or additional sales they receive.","example":"If a campaign reports thousands of impressions and clicks, the advertiser still needs to ask whether the ad appeared on screen, whether the clicks were genuine and whether the campaign caused additional purchases. The reported activity is not the same as verified value.","full":"Tim Hwang's argument that the value of online attention is systematically overstated: a marketplace of fraud and unverifiable prices, while the attention actually bought is worth less and less.\n\nTim Hwang compares parts of the online advertising market with financial markets in which uncertain products are sold as though their value is reliable. An ad may not be seen, a click may be fraudulent, or a purchase may have happened without the ad. The important distinction is between what advertisers can count and the real attention or additional sales they receive.\n\nIf a campaign reports thousands of impressions and clicks, the advertiser still needs to ask whether the ad appeared on screen, whether the clicks were genuine and whether the campaign caused additional purchases. The reported activity is not the same as verified value.","category":"ATTENTION AND DISTRIBUTION"},{"id":24,"term":"Click-through rate (CTR)","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":7,"definition":"The share of people who click after seeing something. The early banner ads of the mid-1990s had a click-through rate of 44 percent; today banner ads command far less attention.","explanation":"CTR compares clicks with opportunities to see or encounter a link or advertisement. For example, 20 clicks from 1,000 impressions gives a CTR of 2%. The lecture uses the striking difference between early banner-ad click","example":"rates and later attention levels to illustrate how online advertising has changed. A click alone does not prove genuine interest or added sales. A campaign with 50 clicks from 2,500 impressions has a CTR of 2 percent. This describes interaction with the advertisement, but it does not by itself tell us how much attention people gave it or whether the campaign produced additional customers.","full":"The share of people who click after seeing something. The early banner ads of the mid-1990s had a click-through rate of 44 percent; today banner ads command far less attention.\n\nCTR compares clicks with opportunities to see or encounter a link or advertisement. For example, 20 clicks from 1,000 impressions gives a CTR of 2%. The lecture uses the striking difference between early banner-ad click\n\nrates and later attention levels to illustrate how online advertising has changed. A click alone does not prove genuine interest or added sales.\n\nA campaign with 50 clicks from 2,500 impressions has a CTR of 2 percent. This describes interaction with the advertisement, but it does not by itself tell us how much attention people gave it or whether the campaign produced additional customers.","category":"ATTENTION AND DISTRIBUTION"},{"id":25,"term":"Search-centric internet","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":8,"definition":"An internet where people reach information by searching and clicking through to websites — the model that answer engines and assistants are now displacing.","explanation":"In this model, a person types a query, reviews search results and opens webpages to find information. Websites therefore compete to be discovered and clicked in those results. Assistant-generated answers challenge that pattern by placing a composed response between the person and the original webpages.","example":"The lecture asks us to consider both the new capability and its consequence for how attention, information or advertising value is distributed online.","full":"An internet where people reach information by searching and clicking through to websites — the model that answer engines and assistants are now displacing.\n\nIn this model, a person types a query, reviews search results and opens webpages to find information. Websites therefore compete to be discovered and clicked in those results. Assistant-generated answers challenge that pattern by placing a composed response between the person and the original webpages.\n\nThe lecture asks us to consider both the new capability and its consequence for how attention, information or advertising value is distributed online.","category":"CONSEQUENCES"},{"id":26,"term":"Replicability","lecture":"Lecture 2 · Web 4.0 and the advertising marketplace","page":8,"definition":"Anything published can be copied and reused in contexts you never chose. What is posted stays: removing the original does not remove the copies.","explanation":"The original publisher cannot reliably control every later copy. A post may be downloaded, screenshot, quoted or shared in a different setting where the context has changed. Deleting the initial version does not automatically remove copies elsewhere.","example":"The lecture asks us to consider both the new capability and its consequence for how attention, information or advertising value is distributed online.","full":"Anything published can be copied and reused in contexts you never chose. What is posted stays: removing the original does not remove the copies.\n\nThe original publisher cannot reliably control every later copy. A post may be downloaded, screenshot, quoted or shared in a different setting where the context has changed. Deleting the initial version does not automatically remove copies elsewhere.\n\nThe lecture asks us to consider both the new capability and its consequence for how attention, information or advertising value is distributed online."},{"id":27,"term":"Objective","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":9,"definition":"A clear, measurable goal that defines what a business wants to achieve with its marketing efforts within a set timeframe. The clearer the objective, the more likely the campaign is to succeed.","explanation":"An objective specifies what an activity is supposed to achieve and by when. A clear objective should be measurable so that the team can judge whether the effort worked. For example, increasing completed sign-ups over a defined campaign period is more actionable than simply wanting more attention.","example":"For example, “increase qualified enquiries through the website within one month” identifies an outcome and a period. “Post more videos” describes an activity rather than a business result. The distinction helps prevent technology or content production from becoming the goal.","full":"A clear, measurable goal that defines what a business wants to achieve with its marketing efforts within a set timeframe. The clearer the objective, the more likely the campaign is to succeed.\n\nAn objective specifies what an activity is supposed to achieve and by when. A clear objective should be measurable so that the team can judge whether the effort worked. For example, increasing completed sign-ups over a defined campaign period is more actionable than simply wanting more attention.\n\nFor example, “increase qualified enquiries through the website within one month” identifies an outcome and a period. “Post more videos” describes an activity rather than a business result. The distinction helps prevent technology or content production from becoming the goal.","category":"OBJECTIVES AND MEASUREMENT"},{"id":28,"term":"Call to action (CTA)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":9,"definition":"What we ask the customer to do next. Every objective needs a CTA: a sign in a store, a sentence from a salesperson, or a clickable link online.","explanation":"A CTA makes the desired next step explicit. It might ask someone to buy, subscribe, download, book or visit a page; it can also exist outside digital marketing. A good CTA connects the communication to the actual objective rather than merely attracting attention.","example":"A registration page might ask visitors to “Sign up,” while a product page might ask them to “Buy now.” A useful CTA makes the next step clear and should follow the objective of the activity rather than being chosen at random.","full":"What we ask the customer to do next. Every objective needs a CTA: a sign in a store, a sentence from a salesperson, or a clickable link online.\n\nA CTA makes the desired next step explicit. It might ask someone to buy, subscribe, download, book or visit a page; it can also exist outside digital marketing. A good CTA connects the communication to the actual objective rather than merely attracting attention.\n\nA registration page might ask visitors to “Sign up,” while a product page might ask them to “Buy now.” A useful CTA makes the next step clear and should follow the objective of the activity rather than being chosen at random.","category":"OBJECTIVES AND MEASUREMENT"},{"id":29,"term":"KPI (key performance indicator)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":9,"definition":"The measure that shows whether an objective is being met — for example conversion rate, cost per acquisition or qualified leads generated.","explanation":"A KPI is selected because it indicates progress toward a specific objective. If the goal is to gain customers, qualified leads or conversion rate may be more useful than follower count. The distinction from a general metric is that a KPI is tied directly to judging success.","example":"If the objective is to generate more purchases, conversion rate or cost per acquisition may be useful KPIs. Total page views might provide context, but a high page-view count does not establish that the purchase objective was achieved.","full":"The measure that shows whether an objective is being met — for example conversion rate, cost per acquisition or qualified leads generated.\n\nA KPI is selected because it indicates progress toward a specific objective. If the goal is to gain customers, qualified leads or conversion rate may be more useful than follower count. The distinction from a general metric is that a KPI is tied directly to judging success.\n\nIf the objective is to generate more purchases, conversion rate or cost per acquisition may be useful KPIs. Total page views might provide context, but a high page-view count does not establish that the purchase objective was achieved.","category":"OBJECTIVES AND MEASUREMENT"},{"id":30,"term":"Metric","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":9,"definition":"Supporting data that describes activity. Metrics explain what happened; KPIs tell you whether the objective was reached.","explanation":"Metrics provide numerical descriptions of what happened, such as impressions, page views or clicks. They help explain activity, but not every metric demonstrates progress toward the campaign objective. Whether a metric is useful as a KPI depends on the goal.","example":"Clicks, impressions and visits are examples of measurements that describe activity. Their meaning depends on the objective. A campaign can attract many visits and still fail if the visitors do not complete the intended action.","full":"Supporting data that describes activity. Metrics explain what happened; KPIs tell you whether the objective was reached.\n\nMetrics provide numerical descriptions of what happened, such as impressions, page views or clicks. They help explain activity, but not every metric demonstrates progress toward the campaign objective. Whether a metric is useful as a KPI depends on the goal.\n\nClicks, impressions and visits are examples of measurements that describe activity. Their meaning depends on the objective. A campaign can attract many visits and still fail if the visitors do not complete the intended action.","category":"OBJECTIVES AND MEASUREMENT"},{"id":31,"term":"Vanity metric","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":10,"definition":"A comfort goal that looks good but says nothing about the objective — followers, total page views, app downloads without active users, subscribers without open rates.","explanation":"Large follower counts or page-view totals can look impressive without showing meaningful outcomes. A company might attract many visits without receiving sign-ups or sales. The lecture warns marketers not to confuse numbers that feel successful with measurements that answer the actual objective.","example":"For instance, an account gaining followers might look successful even when very few followers interact or become customers. The course warns against confusing impressive-looking counts with evidence that an actual objective has been met.","full":"A comfort goal that looks good but says nothing about the objective — followers, total page views, app downloads without active users, subscribers without open rates.\n\nLarge follower counts or page-view totals can look impressive without showing meaningful outcomes. A company might attract many visits without receiving sign-ups or sales. The lecture warns marketers not to confuse numbers that feel successful with measurements that answer the actual objective.\n\nFor instance, an account gaining followers might look successful even when very few followers interact or become customers. The course warns against confusing impressive-looking counts with evidence that an actual objective has been met.","category":"OBJECTIVES AND MEASUREMENT"},{"id":32,"term":"Marketing funnel","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":10,"definition":"A metaphor describing the customer journey from first awareness to loyal customer. It helps marketers set clear objectives for each stage so activities match where customers are in their decision-making.","explanation":"The funnel represents different stages in the customer’s decision-making process. An unfamiliar customer needs different communication from someone already evaluating alternatives or ready to buy. The model helps connect each stage to suitable activities, calls to action and objectives.","example":"A person discovering a product for the first time needs different information from someone who is almost ready to purchase. The funnel helps explain why marketers should not expect every communication to produce the same immediate action.","full":"A metaphor describing the customer journey from first awareness to loyal customer. It helps marketers set clear objectives for each stage so activities match where customers are in their decision-making.\n\nThe funnel represents different stages in the customer’s decision-making process. An unfamiliar customer needs different communication from someone already evaluating alternatives or ready to buy. The model helps connect each stage to suitable activities, calls to action and objectives.\n\nA person discovering a product for the first time needs different information from someone who is almost ready to purchase. The funnel helps explain why marketers should not expect every communication to produce the same immediate action.","category":"FUNNELS"},{"id":33,"term":"AIDA","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":10,"definition":"The mother funnel: Attention, Interest, Desire, Action. There is a CTA in each step.","explanation":"Attention is the stage where someone notices an offer, Interest where they begin engaging with it, Desire where they want it, and Action where they take the requested step. The lecture emphasises a CTA at every stage. AIDA offers a simple way to connect a message to the next desired behaviour.","example":"An advertisement may first capture attention, then create interest, encourage desire and finally direct a person towards action. The model describes steps in communication and helps clarify what the marketer is asking at each stage.","full":"The mother funnel: Attention, Interest, Desire, Action. There is a CTA in each step.\n\nAttention is the stage where someone notices an offer, Interest where they begin engaging with it, Desire where they want it, and Action where they take the requested step. The lecture emphasises a CTA at every stage. AIDA offers a simple way to connect a message to the next desired behaviour.\n\nAn advertisement may first capture attention, then create interest, encourage desire and finally direct a person towards action. The model describes steps in communication and helps clarify what the marketer is asking at each stage.","category":"FUNNELS"},{"id":34,"term":"The digital marketing funnel","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":10,"definition":"The funnel as most often used in digital marketing: awareness, interest, consideration, intent, evaluation, purchase.","explanation":"The six stages make the customer’s progression more detailed than AIDA: awareness, interest, consideration, intent, evaluation and purchase. A person may first discover a product, then compare it with alternatives and finally make a decision. Marketers use the stages to choose appropriate information and objectives rather than","example":"treating every visitor as ready to buy. For example, someone can first encounter a brand, become interested, compare options, develop an intention to buy, evaluate the offer and eventually complete a purchase. Each stage calls for information that suits that point in the decision process.","full":"The funnel as most often used in digital marketing: awareness, interest, consideration, intent, evaluation, purchase.\n\nThe six stages make the customer’s progression more detailed than AIDA: awareness, interest, consideration, intent, evaluation and purchase. A person may first discover a product, then compare it with alternatives and finally make a decision. Marketers use the stages to choose appropriate information and objectives rather than\n\ntreating every visitor as ready to buy.\n\nFor example, someone can first encounter a brand, become interested, compare options, develop an intention to buy, evaluate the offer and eventually complete a purchase. Each stage calls for information that suits that point in the decision process.","category":"FUNNELS"},{"id":35,"term":"Conversion","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":11,"definition":"The point where a visitor completes the action the objective asks for — a purchase, a sign-up, a download.","explanation":"A conversion occurs when the user completes the desired action, not merely when they see the content. Depending on the objective, that action might be a purchase, sign-up or download. The definition of a conversion therefore depends on what the business asked the visitor to do.","example":"For one campaign, a completed purchase is the conversion; for another, submitting an enquiry form is the conversion. The event must be defined in relation to the objective rather than assuming that every click counts as success.","full":"The point where a visitor completes the action the objective asks for — a purchase, a sign-up, a download.\n\nA conversion occurs when the user completes the desired action, not merely when they see the content. Depending on the objective, that action might be a purchase, sign-up or download. The definition of a conversion therefore depends on what the business asked the visitor to do.\n\nFor one campaign, a completed purchase is the conversion; for another, submitting an enquiry form is the conversion. The event must be defined in relation to the objective rather than assuming that every click counts as success.","category":"OBJECTIVES AND MEASUREMENT"},{"id":36,"term":"Jobs to be done (JTBD)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":11,"definition":"Customers “hire” products and services to do a job; they seek to make progress in a specific circumstance. Jobs are functional, emotional and social, and depend on context rather than demographics.","explanation":"JTBD begins with the progress a person is trying to make in a particular situation, rather than beginning with age or other demographic categories. A job may be functional, such as accomplishing a task; emotional, such as feeling reassured; or social, such as being perceived in a certain way. A product succeeds when it helps the customer make the progress they need.","example":"A student considering a programme may have a functional job of finding the right information, an emotional job of feeling confident and a social job connected with how the choice is perceived. Looking only at age or location would miss these motivations.","full":"Customers “hire” products and services to do a job; they seek to make progress in a specific circumstance. Jobs are functional, emotional and social, and depend on context rather than demographics.\n\nJTBD begins with the progress a person is trying to make in a particular situation, rather than beginning with age or other demographic categories. A job may be functional, such as accomplishing a task; emotional, such as feeling reassured; or social, such as being perceived in a certain way. A product succeeds when it helps the customer make the progress they need.\n\nA student considering a programme may have a functional job of finding the right information, an emotional job of feeling confident and a social job connected with how the choice is perceived. Looking only at age or location would miss these motivations.","category":"CUSTOMER BEHAVIOUR"},{"id":37,"term":"Job mapping","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":11,"definition":"Breaking a job down into its steps — define, locate, prepare, execute, conclude — to see where customers struggle and where the innovation opportunities are.","explanation":"Mapping a job divides it into define, locate, prepare, execute and conclude. This makes it possible to identify where the customer gets stuck rather than assuming the difficulty happens only during the final action. Each problem point may suggest an opportunity to improve the offer or information.","example":"For example, a customer may know what they want but struggle to locate the relevant information or complete the final step. Mapping the job identifies that specific obstacle, which is more actionable than treating the whole customer experience as a single activity.","full":"Breaking a job down into its steps — define, locate, prepare, execute, conclude — to see where customers struggle and where the innovation opportunities are.\n\nMapping a job divides it into define, locate, prepare, execute and conclude. This makes it possible to identify where the customer gets stuck rather than assuming the difficulty happens only during the final action. Each problem point may suggest an opportunity to improve the offer or information.\n\nFor example, a customer may know what they want but struggle to locate the relevant information or complete the final step. Mapping the job identifies that specific obstacle, which is more actionable than treating the whole customer experience as a single activity.","category":"CUSTOMER BEHAVIOUR"},{"id":38,"term":"Inbound marketing","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":11,"definition":"Marketing that earns attention by being findable and useful: pages, guides and blog posts that match real searches, organic social, newsletters people choose to receive, reviews and user-generated content.","explanation":"Inbound activity aims to be found when people have a relevant question or need. Search-friendly guides, helpful pages, chosen newsletters and organic content can attract attention without directly interrupting the user. The emphasis is on being useful and discoverable.","example":"A useful guide that answers a real search question can bring a potential customer to a website without interrupting their activity. The value of the content and its findability are what attract attention.","full":"Marketing that earns attention by being findable and useful: pages, guides and blog posts that match real searches, organic social, newsletters people choose to receive, reviews and user-generated content.\n\nInbound activity aims to be found when people have a relevant question or need. Search-friendly guides, helpful pages, chosen newsletters and organic content can attract attention without directly interrupting the user. The emphasis is on being useful and discoverable.\n\nA useful guide that answers a real search question can bring a potential customer to a website without interrupting their activity. The value of the content and its findability are what attract attention.","category":"INBOUND AND OUTBOUND"},{"id":39,"term":"Outbound marketing","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":12,"definition":"Marketing that pushes a message to an audience: paid social ads, display banners and pop-ups, pre-roll video, direct mail, cold calls, out-of-home advertising and sponsored content.","explanation":"Outbound activity sends the message toward an audience rather than waiting to be found. Examples in the lecture include paid social advertising, banners, video pre-rolls, direct mail and cold calls. The key contrast with inbound is the way attention is reached: interruption or push versus attraction through useful content.","example":"A display banner or pre-roll advertisement delivers a message while someone is doing something else. The marketer initiates the contact rather than waiting for the audience to search for the message.","full":"Marketing that pushes a message to an audience: paid social ads, display banners and pop-ups, pre-roll video, direct mail, cold calls, out-of-home advertising and sponsored content.\n\nOutbound activity sends the message toward an audience rather than waiting to be found. Examples in the lecture include paid social advertising, banners, video pre-rolls, direct mail and cold calls. The key contrast with inbound is the way attention is reached: interruption or push versus attraction through useful content.\n\nA display banner or pre-roll advertisement delivers a message while someone is doing something else. The marketer initiates the contact rather than waiting for the audience to search for the message.","category":"INBOUND AND OUTBOUND"},{"id":40,"term":"SEO (search engine optimization)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":12,"definition":"The work of making a site findable and rankable in search engines. It has three components: technical, on-site and off-site.","explanation":"SEO seeks to make pages accessible, relevant and credible in organic search results. Technical SEO helps search engines reach and read a site; on-site SEO improves the pages themselves; off-site SEO concerns signals from elsewhere. These aspects work together to support findability and ranking.","example":"A page can have useful content but still be difficult for a search engine to reach, understand or trust. Technical, on-site and off-site work address different parts of that problem, which is why SEO is not just adding keywords.","full":"The work of making a site findable and rankable in search engines. It has three components: technical, on-site and off-site.\n\nSEO seeks to make pages accessible, relevant and credible in organic search results. Technical SEO helps search engines reach and read a site; on-site SEO improves the pages themselves; off-site SEO concerns signals from elsewhere. These aspects work together to support findability and ranking.\n\nA page can have useful content but still be difficult for a search engine to reach, understand or trust. Technical, on-site and off-site work address different parts of that problem, which is why SEO is not just adding keywords.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":41,"term":"SERP (search engine results page)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":12,"definition":"The page a search engine returns for a query. One query produces many components: AI overview, ads, organic results, local pack, knowledge panel, image and video blocks, and more.","explanation":"A SERP is more than a simple list of blue links. Depending on the query it may include advertisements, ordinary organic listings, an AI Overview, a featured snippet, a map and local listings, images or a knowledge panel. Understanding the result layout helps explain how people discover information.","example":"Someone searching for a local service may see advertisements, map listings and organic pages on the same results screen. Understanding the SERP requires recognising these different result types rather than assuming every visible item is an ordinary blue link.","full":"The page a search engine returns for a query. One query produces many components: AI overview, ads, organic results, local pack, knowledge panel, image and video blocks, and more.\n\nA SERP is more than a simple list of blue links. Depending on the query it may include advertisements, ordinary organic listings, an AI Overview, a featured snippet, a map and local listings, images or a knowledge panel. Understanding the result layout helps explain how people discover information.\n\nSomeone searching for a local service may see advertisements, map listings and organic pages on the same results screen. Understanding the SERP requires recognising these different result types rather than assuming every visible item is an ordinary blue link.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":42,"term":"Crawler (robot)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":12,"definition":"The robots that work their way across the internet, following links and reading content so the search engine can index it.","explanation":"A crawler follows links and reads accessible pages so the search engine can discover content. This happens before the page can be included in a searchable index. Poor linking or technical barriers may prevent important pages from being discovered.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The robots that work their way across the internet, following links and reading content so the search engine can index it.\n\nA crawler follows links and reads accessible pages so the search engine can discover content. This happens before the page can be included in a searchable index. Poor linking or technical barriers may prevent important pages from being discovered.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":43,"term":"Index","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":13,"definition":"The search engine's record of the pages its robots have found. Each search engine has an index of websites that the robots keep updated.","explanation":"The index is a search engine’s stored record of discovered pages. Crawling finds and reads pages; indexing makes the information available to the search system. A page that is not properly indexed cannot compete normally in search results.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The search engine's record of the pages its robots have found. Each search engine has an index of websites that the robots keep updated.\n\nThe index is a search engine’s stored record of discovered pages. Crawling finds and reads pages; indexing makes the information available to the search system. A page that is not properly indexed cannot compete normally in search results.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":44,"term":"Algorithm","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":13,"definition":"The search engine's code that decides how the pages in the index are ranked for a given query.","explanation":"Once pages are in the index, the search engine must decide which ones best answer a query. The algorithm provides that ranking process. SEO work aims to make a page accessible, relevant and useful within that system, rather than directly controlling the rankings.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The search engine's code that decides how the pages in the index are ranked for a given query.\n\nOnce pages are in the index, the search engine must decide which ones best answer a query. The algorithm provides that ranking process. SEO work aims to make a page accessible, relevant and useful within that system, rather than directly controlling the rankings.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":45,"term":"Organic result","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":13,"definition":"A standard, unpaid listing on the SERP — title, URL and snippet — possibly with sitelinks or a site search box for brands.","explanation":"An organic listing appears without the site paying for that particular placement. It commonly includes a title, URL and text snippet and can sometimes include sitelinks. Organic visibility is therefore different from paid search advertising.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"A standard, unpaid listing on the SERP — title, URL and snippet — possibly with sitelinks or a site search box for brands.\n\nAn organic listing appears without the site paying for that particular placement. It commonly includes a title, URL and text snippet and can sometimes include sitelinks. Organic visibility is therefore different from paid search advertising.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":46,"term":"Position 0 (featured snippet)","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":13,"definition":"An answer box extracted from a page — a paragraph, list, table or video — shown ahead of the regular results.","explanation":"A featured snippet pulls a short answer from a page and presents it prominently above regular results. The answer may be formatted as a paragraph, list, table or video. It demonstrates how a search engine can provide information directly on its results page.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"An answer box extracted from a page — a paragraph, list, table or video — shown ahead of the regular results.\n\nA featured snippet pulls a short answer from a page and presents it prominently above regular results. The answer may be formatted as a paragraph, list, table or video. It demonstrates how a search engine can provide information directly on its results page.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":47,"term":"AI Overview","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":14,"definition":"An AI-generated snapshot at the top of the SERP, with cited links.","explanation":"An AI Overview provides an AI-generated summary within the search results and may include links to cited sources. It changes the search experience because the user may receive an answer before clicking a webpage. This is one reason the course discusses GEO alongside SEO.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"An AI-generated snapshot at the top of the SERP, with cited links.\n\nAn AI Overview provides an AI-generated summary within the search results and may include links to cited sources. It changes the search experience because the user may receive an answer before clicking a webpage. This is one reason the course discusses GEO alongside SEO.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":48,"term":"Knowledge panel","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":14,"definition":"A panel of facts about a brand, person or place, shown at the top or on the right of the results page.","explanation":"A knowledge panel collects key facts about a recognised person, place or brand into a dedicated result feature. It is different from an ordinary organic webpage listing. The feature can help users identify an entity and find basic information quickly.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"A panel of facts about a brand, person or place, shown at the top or on the right of the results page.\n\nA knowledge panel collects key facts about a recognised person, place or brand into a dedicated result feature. It is different from an ordinary organic webpage listing. The feature can help users identify an entity and find basic information quickly.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":49,"term":"Local pack","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":14,"definition":"A map with three local listings showing ratings, opening hours and directions.","explanation":"The local pack combines a map with a small set of nearby business listings. Information such as ratings, hours and directions helps a person choose where to go. It is particularly relevant when the search has a local or place-based need.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"A map with three local listings showing ratings, opening hours and directions.\n\nThe local pack combines a map with a small set of nearby business listings. Information such as ratings, hours and directions helps a person choose where to go. It is particularly relevant when the search has a local or place-based need.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":50,"term":"Search intent","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":14,"definition":"What the person actually wants from a query. Four types: informational, navigational, commercial (investigation) and transactional — each mapping to a stage of the funnel.","explanation":"Search intent is the purpose behind a query rather than just the words typed. The four types in the course are informational, navigational, commercial and transactional. Understanding the difference helps a marketer match a page and its content to the action the searcher actually wants.","example":"A query asking “what is SEO?” needs an explanation; a query asking for a particular website needs a destination; a comparison query needs help evaluating options; and a query to buy needs a way to act. The content should match the purpose.","full":"What the person actually wants from a query. Four types: informational, navigational, commercial (investigation) and transactional — each mapping to a stage of the funnel.\n\nSearch intent is the purpose behind a query rather than just the words typed. The four types in the course are informational, navigational, commercial and transactional. Understanding the difference helps a marketer match a page and its content to the action the searcher actually wants.\n\nA query asking “what is SEO?” needs an explanation; a query asking for a particular website needs a destination; a comparison query needs help evaluating options; and a query to buy needs a way to act. The content should match the purpose.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":51,"term":"Informational intent","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":15,"definition":"The user is looking to learn or understand something — answers, how-tos, facts. Maps to awareness and problem definition.","explanation":"The searcher wants to understand a topic or solve a question, often through a guide or explanation. A page that answers clearly is more appropriate than a checkout page. In the course, this often connects to awareness and defining a problem.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The user is looking to learn or understand something — answers, how-tos, facts. Maps to awareness and problem definition.\n\nThe searcher wants to understand a topic or solve a question, often through a guide or explanation. A page that answers clearly is more appropriate than a checkout page. In the course, this often connects to awareness and defining a problem.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":52,"term":"Navigational intent","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":15,"definition":"The user is trying to reach a specific site or page: a brand homepage, login or store locator. Maps to consideration and retention.","explanation":"The searcher already knows the particular website or destination they want to reach. A branded search or login-page search is an example. The best response makes that destination easy to find instead of giving an unrelated general explanation.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The user is trying to reach a specific site or page: a brand homepage, login or store locator. Maps to consideration and retention.\n\nThe searcher already knows the particular website or destination they want to reach. A branded search or login-page search is an example. The best response makes that destination easy to find instead of giving an unrelated general explanation.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":53,"term":"Commercial intent","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":15,"definition":"The user is comparing options before a decision — reviews, comparisons, case studies. Maps to consideration and evaluation.","explanation":"The user is investigating options before deciding, for example through reviews, comparisons or case studies. Content should help evaluate alternatives and resolve uncertainties. This differs from transactional intent because the final action has not yet been chosen.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The user is comparing options before a decision — reviews, comparisons, case studies. Maps to consideration and evaluation.\n\nThe user is investigating options before deciding, for example through reviews, comparisons or case studies. Content should help evaluate alternatives and resolve uncertainties. This differs from transactional intent because the final action has not yet been chosen.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":54,"term":"Transactional intent","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":15,"definition":"The user is ready to act now: buy, sign up or download. Maps to conversion and purchase.","explanation":"The person is ready to do something, such as purchase, download or register. The page should make the action clear and straightforward. It belongs close to conversion and purchase in the funnel.","example":"In practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","full":"The user is ready to act now: buy, sign up or download. Maps to conversion and purchase.\n\nThe person is ready to do something, such as purchase, download or register. The page should make the action clear and straightforward. It belongs close to conversion and purchase in the funnel.\n\nIn practice, the meaning of this concept depends on the user’s goal and the specific action or information being considered, rather than on an impressive number alone.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":55,"term":"SEO audit","lecture":"Lecture 3 · Digital marketing fundamentals and SEO basics","page":15,"definition":"An SEO analysis of a site that uncovers the highest-priority issues: a Site Health rank, errors and warnings, crawlability, HTTPS, Core Web Vitals, internal linking, markup and site performance.","explanation":"An SEO audit identifies issues that could limit a website’s search performance. Findings can cover site health, crawling, security, page experience, links and markup. The point is to prioritise what to investigate and fix, not just produce a long list of technical observations.","example":"An audit helps a team decide which problems deserve attention first. It is more useful to identify a serious crawlability issue and its consequence than to present a long undifferentiated list of warnings.","full":"An SEO analysis of a site that uncovers the highest-priority issues: a Site Health rank, errors and warnings, crawlability, HTTPS, Core Web Vitals, internal linking, markup and site performance.\n\nAn SEO audit identifies issues that could limit a website’s search performance. Findings can cover site health, crawling, security, page experience, links and markup. The point is to prioritise what to investigate and fix, not just produce a long list of technical observations.\n\nAn audit helps a team decide which problems deserve attention first. It is more useful to identify a serious crawlability issue and its consequence than to present a long undifferentiated list of warnings.","category":"SEO BASICS"},{"id":56,"term":"Technical SEO","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":17,"definition":"The “under-the-hood” aspects of a site that affect crawling, indexation, user experience and rankings. It asks one question: can a machine reach, read and trust your site?.","explanation":"Technical SEO focuses on whether the website works in ways that allow crawlers and users to reach its content. It includes crawlability, secure connections, performance, mobile usability and site structure. A page with excellent writing can still struggle if it cannot be reached or processed properly.","example":"For example, broken links, inaccessible pages or a poor mobile experience can obstruct users or search-engine systems even when the content itself is good. Technical SEO addresses those barriers.","full":"The “under-the-hood” aspects of a site that affect crawling, indexation, user experience and rankings. It asks one question: can a machine reach, read and trust your site?.\n\nTechnical SEO focuses on whether the website works in ways that allow crawlers and users to reach its content. It includes crawlability, secure connections, performance, mobile usability and site structure. A page with excellent writing can still struggle if it cannot be reached or processed properly.\n\nFor example, broken links, inaccessible pages or a poor mobile experience can obstruct users or search-engine systems even when the content itself is good. Technical SEO addresses those barriers.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":57,"term":"On-site SEO","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":17,"definition":"Everything on your own pages that you control: content, keywords, titles, descriptions, headings, URLs, images and links.","explanation":"This covers elements the site owner can edit directly, including page content, headings, metadata, descriptive URLs, image text and internal links. The goal is to make each page understandable and useful for its intended query. It complements technical access and signals from other websites.","example":"For example, a page with a clear title, logical headings, useful copy and descriptive internal links helps both readers and search engines understand the subject and find related information.","full":"Everything on your own pages that you control: content, keywords, titles, descriptions, headings, URLs, images and links.\n\nThis covers elements the site owner can edit directly, including page content, headings, metadata, descriptive URLs, image text and internal links. The goal is to make each page understandable and useful for its intended query. It complements technical access and signals from other websites.\n\nFor example, a page with a clear title, logical headings, useful copy and descriptive internal links helps both readers and search engines understand the subject and find related information.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":58,"term":"Off-site SEO","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":17,"definition":"Everything said about you somewhere else — business profiles, reviews, mentions, backlinks, PR and word of mouth. You influence it; you do not control it.","explanation":"Off-site factors are created outside the website itself, such as backlinks, business listings, reviews and mentions. A company can influence these through its work and relationships but cannot completely control them. The lecture connects them with credibility and recognition beyond the site.","example":"For example, links from other relevant websites and public reviews can help people discover a business outside its own pages. The business can encourage good relationships and useful coverage, but cannot fully dictate what others say.","full":"Everything said about you somewhere else — business profiles, reviews, mentions, backlinks, PR and word of mouth. You influence it; you do not control it.\n\nOff-site factors are created outside the website itself, such as backlinks, business listings, reviews and mentions. A company can influence these through its work and relationships but cannot completely control them. The lecture connects them with credibility and recognition beyond the site.\n\nFor example, links from other relevant websites and public reviews can help people discover a business outside its own pages. The business can encourage good relationships and useful coverage, but cannot fully dictate what others say.","category":"OFF-SITE SEO"},{"id":59,"term":"Sitemap","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":17,"definition":"A simple display of the entirety of your site, telling search engines which pages exist.","explanation":"A sitemap helps search engines discover the pages a site wants them to know about. It is particularly useful as a list of available URLs, complementing links between pages. Having a sitemap does not replace the need for sensible site structure.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"A simple display of the entirety of your site, telling search engines which pages exist.\n\nA sitemap helps search engines discover the pages a site wants them to know about. It is particularly useful as a list of available URLs, complementing links between pages. Having a sitemap does not replace the need for sensible site structure.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":60,"term":"Site hierarchy","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":18,"definition":"The structure of your pages within the site. The hierarchy tells the crawler what belongs to what — and what matters most.","explanation":"Hierarchy organises pages into understandable relationships. It shows which information is broad, which is more specific and how pages connect. A clear hierarchy helps both visitors and crawlers navigate the website.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"The structure of your pages within the site. The hierarchy tells the crawler what belongs to what — and what matters most.\n\nHierarchy organises pages into understandable relationships. It shows which information is broad, which is more specific and how pages connect. A clear hierarchy helps both visitors and crawlers navigate the website.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":61,"term":"Metadata","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":18,"definition":"Code that tells the robots about your site and its pages.","explanation":"Metadata provides information about a page beyond its visible body copy. The course discusses titles and descriptions because they help describe pages to search systems and may influence how a result is presented. Each page should have metadata suited to its own content.","example":"For example, a page title and description can indicate the subject of a page and influence how it is represented in search. The page owner can supply these elements, although a search engine may choose different text in its result.","full":"Code that tells the robots about your site and its pages.\n\nMetadata provides information about a page beyond its visible body copy. The course discusses titles and descriptions because they help describe pages to search systems and may influence how a result is presented. Each page should have metadata suited to its own content.\n\nFor example, a page title and description can indicate the subject of a page and influence how it is represented in search. The page owner can supply these elements, although a search engine may choose different text in its result.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":62,"term":"Meta title","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":18,"definition":"The clickable headline on the SERP. Concise, relevant and containing important keywords — around 50–60 characters. One unique title per page.","explanation":"The title communicates the page topic in a short, distinctive form and commonly appears as the clickable search-result heading. The course recommends approximately 50–60 characters and a relevant keyword. Giving each page its own title helps distinguish the pages in search.","example":"For example, a specific page title describing the actual subject is more useful than a vague label repeated across every page. It gives readers a clearer idea of the destination before they click.","full":"The clickable headline on the SERP. Concise, relevant and containing important keywords — around 50–60 characters. One unique title per page.\n\nThe title communicates the page topic in a short, distinctive form and commonly appears as the clickable search-result heading. The course recommends approximately 50–60 characters and a relevant keyword. Giving each page its own title helps distinguish the pages in search.\n\nFor example, a specific page title describing the actual subject is more useful than a vague label repeated across every page. It gives readers a clearer idea of the destination before they click.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":63,"term":"Meta description","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":18,"definition":"The brief summary below the meta title on the SERP, around 150–160 characters. It is the sales pitch: it should give a reason to click, not list keywords.","explanation":"The description briefly explains why a searcher might want to open the page. The lecture suggests around 150–160 characters and stresses persuasion rather than keyword stuffing. It should accurately describe the page and make its relevance clear.","example":"A description should communicate why a particular result is relevant to the query. Simply repeating keywords offers little reason to visit; the lecture treats the description as a short persuasive summary.","full":"The brief summary below the meta title on the SERP, around 150–160 characters. It is the sales pitch: it should give a reason to click, not list keywords.\n\nThe description briefly explains why a searcher might want to open the page. The lecture suggests around 150–160 characters and stresses persuasion rather than keyword stuffing. It should accurately describe the page and make its relevance clear.\n\nA description should communicate why a particular result is relevant to the query. Simply repeating keywords offers little reason to visit; the lecture treats the description as a short persuasive summary.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":64,"term":"Header tags (H1, H2, H3)","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":18,"definition":"The outline of the page. H1 is the main title; H2 and H3 are subheadings. They tell both reader and crawler what the page is about, in order of importance.","explanation":"Heading tags form an outline of the information on a page. The H1 names the main subject, and H2/H3 headings break it into logically organised sections. This helps people scan the page and helps crawlers interpret its structure.","example":"Think of a page as an outline: the H1 names the main subject, H2 headings organise its major sections, and H3 headings divide those sections when necessary. Skipping structure makes the page harder to follow.","full":"The outline of the page. H1 is the main title; H2 and H3 are subheadings. They tell both reader and crawler what the page is about, in order of importance.\n\nHeading tags form an outline of the information on a page. The H1 names the main subject, and H2/H3 headings break it into logically organised sections. This helps people scan the page and helps crawlers interpret its structure.\n\nThink of a page as an outline: the H1 names the main subject, H2 headings organise its major sections, and H3 headings divide those sections when necessary. Skipping structure makes the page harder to follow.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":65,"term":"URL slug","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":19,"definition":"The specific address of a page. It should be clean, descriptive and readable: /womens/running-shoes tells you where you are, /p?id=99381 tells you nothing.","explanation":"A descriptive slug makes a page’s topic and location easier to understand from its address. For example, /womens/running-shoes gives more information than /p?id=99381. Clear URLs support understandable navigation rather than hiding the subject behind an arbitrary identifier.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"The specific address of a page. It should be clean, descriptive and readable: /womens/running-shoes tells you where you are, /p?id=99381 tells you nothing.\n\nA descriptive slug makes a page’s topic and location easier to understand from its address. For example, /womens/running-shoes gives more information than /p?id=99381. Clear URLs support understandable navigation rather than hiding the subject behind an arbitrary identifier.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":66,"term":"Alt text","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":19,"definition":"A description of an image for search engines and for people using a screen reader. Search engines cannot see your images — alt text is how you describe them.","explanation":"Alt text describes an image in words, particularly for visitors using screen readers and for systems interpreting the image. It should communicate what is meaningful about the image in its context. This is both a content-accessibility consideration and an on-site SEO element.","example":"For instance, useful alt text describes the information a meaningful image contributes instead of merely saying “image.” This supports people using screen readers and helps explain visual content.","full":"A description of an image for search engines and for people using a screen reader. Search engines cannot see your images — alt text is how you describe them.\n\nAlt text describes an image in words, particularly for visitors using screen readers and for systems interpreting the image. It should communicate what is meaningful about the image in its context. This is both a content-accessibility consideration and an on-site SEO element.\n\nFor instance, useful alt text describes the information a meaningful image contributes instead of merely saying “image.” This supports people using screen readers and helps explain visual content.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":67,"term":"Internal link","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":19,"definition":"A link to another page on the same site. Internal links pass value between your own pages and keep the visitor moving through the funnel.","explanation":"Internal links connect relevant pages belonging to the same website. They help visitors move between related information and allow crawlers to discover the site’s structure. They can also guide someone from an introductory page toward more detailed content.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"A link to another page on the same site. Internal links pass value between your own pages and keep the visitor moving through the funnel.\n\nInternal links connect relevant pages belonging to the same website. They help visitors move between related information and allow crawlers to discover the site’s structure. They can also guide someone from an introductory page toward more detailed content.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":68,"term":"External link (outbound link)","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":19,"definition":"A link pointing to an authoritative, relevant source outside your own website. It builds credibility and provides value to your users.","explanation":"An outbound link takes the reader to a relevant source on another website. It can provide supporting information that the current page does not contain. The lecture presents relevant authoritative links as a way to add value","example":"and credibility for the reader. This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"A link pointing to an authoritative, relevant source outside your own website. It builds credibility and provides value to your users.\n\nAn outbound link takes the reader to a relevant source on another website. It can provide supporting information that the current page does not contain. The lecture presents relevant authoritative links as a way to add value\n\nand credibility for the reader.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":69,"term":"Backlink","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":20,"definition":"A link from someone else's site to yours. Search engines read it as a vote: someone thought this was worth pointing at. One link from a trusted site beats a hundred from nobody.","explanation":"A backlink is created when another website links to yours. It can suggest that the linked information is worth referring to. The course stresses the quality and trustworthiness of the referring site rather than simply the number of links.","example":"A mention with a link from an established, relevant source is different from dozens of links from unrelated or low-quality pages. The lecture uses backlinks to show how other sites can signal that material is worth finding.","full":"A link from someone else's site to yours. Search engines read it as a vote: someone thought this was worth pointing at. One link from a trusted site beats a hundred from nobody.\n\nA backlink is created when another website links to yours. It can suggest that the linked information is worth referring to. The course stresses the quality and trustworthiness of the referring site rather than simply the number of links.\n\nA mention with a link from an established, relevant source is different from dozens of links from unrelated or low-quality pages. The lecture uses backlinks to show how other sites can signal that material is worth finding.","category":"OFF-SITE SEO"},{"id":70,"term":"Sitelinks","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":20,"definition":"Additional links to important pages that Google adds beneath the main result — typically for well-structured sites with clear navigation. They increase visibility and click-through rate.","explanation":"Sitelinks are extra destinations shown below a main search result, often for well-organised websites. They make it easier to jump to important sections without first opening the homepage. Their appearance can increase the space and visibility of a listing in results.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"Additional links to important pages that Google adds beneath the main result — typically for well-structured sites with clear navigation. They increase visibility and click-through rate.\n\nSitelinks are extra destinations shown below a main search result, often for well-organised websites. They make it easier to jump to important sections without first opening the homepage. Their appearance can increase the space and visibility of a listing in results.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"ON-SITE SEO"},{"id":71,"term":"Google Business Profile","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":20,"definition":"A company's profile in Google's search and maps results — address, opening hours, photos, category. The cheapest visibility a company can get, as long as it is kept up to date.","explanation":"This profile can display a business’s location, category, photos and opening hours in Google Search and Maps. Accurate information helps people find and assess the business. The lecture emphasises keeping the listing up to date.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"A company's profile in Google's search and maps results — address, opening hours, photos, category. The cheapest visibility a company can get, as long as it is kept up to date.\n\nThis profile can display a business’s location, category, photos and opening hours in Google Search and Maps. Accurate information helps people find and assess the business. The lecture emphasises keeping the listing up to date.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"OFF-SITE SEO"},{"id":72,"term":"Reviews and mentions","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":20,"definition":"Other people doing your marketing. Answering them matters, because the reply is read by everyone who comes after.","explanation":"People’s comments, recommendations and discussions can influence the reputation of a business. The organisation does not fully control this conversation, but it can respond. A public reply matters because later readers see both the original comment and the response.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"Other people doing your marketing. Answering them matters, because the reply is read by everyone who comes after.\n\nPeople’s comments, recommendations and discussions can influence the reputation of a business. The organisation does not fully control this conversation, but it can respond. A public reply matters because later readers see both the original comment and the response.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"OFF-SITE SEO"},{"id":73,"term":"HTTPS","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":21,"definition":"Secure communication between the browser and the site. Without it, browsers warn the visitor before they ever see your content.","explanation":"HTTPS creates a secure connection between the visitor’s browser and the website. The course includes it as a basic technical SEO and trust requirement. A browser warning about an insecure site can damage confidence before visitors even reach the intended content.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"Secure communication between the browser and the site. Without it, browsers warn the visitor before they ever see your content.\n\nHTTPS creates a secure connection between the visitor’s browser and the website. The course includes it as a basic technical SEO and trust requirement. A browser warning about an insecure site can damage confidence before visitors even reach the intended content.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":74,"term":"Core Web Vitals","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":21,"definition":"User-focused metrics designed to measure page quality in terms of a smooth and seamless user experience. This is how speed is measured.","explanation":"These measurements focus on aspects of the real page experience, particularly how smoothly the site behaves for users. They help turn the general idea of performance into measurable signals. Poor performance can make a page harder and more frustrating to use.","example":"A page can contain the right information but still frustrate visitors if loading or interaction is poor. These user-focused measures help identify whether the experience feels smooth rather than judging the page only by its content.","full":"User-focused metrics designed to measure page quality in terms of a smooth and seamless user experience. This is how speed is measured.\n\nThese measurements focus on aspects of the real page experience, particularly how smoothly the site behaves for users. They help turn the general idea of performance into measurable signals. Poor performance can make a page harder and more frustrating to use.\n\nA page can contain the right information but still frustrate visitors if loading or interaction is poor. These user-focused measures help identify whether the experience feels smooth rather than judging the page only by its content.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":75,"term":"Mobile-friendliness","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":21,"definition":"Whether the site adapts well across screen sizes. Google evaluates the mobile version of your site first.","explanation":"A mobile-friendly page adapts to small screens so visitors can read and use it. The course connects this to Google’s emphasis on evaluating the mobile version of a site. A design that works on a desktop but fails on a phone creates both usability and search problems.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"Whether the site adapts well across screen sizes. Google evaluates the mobile version of your site first.\n\nA mobile-friendly page adapts to small screens so visitors can read and use it. The course connects this to Google’s emphasis on evaluating the mobile version of a site. A design that works on a desktop but fails on a phone creates both usability and search problems.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":76,"term":"404 error (broken link)","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":21,"definition":"A missing page. 404 errors waste both the visitor's time and the crawler's budget.","explanation":"A 404 occurs when a requested page is missing. A visitor may reach a dead end, and a crawler may spend effort following a link that provides no useful page. Site audits identify these errors so important navigation paths can be repaired.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"A missing page. 404 errors waste both the visitor's time and the crawler's budget.\n\nA 404 occurs when a requested page is missing. A visitor may reach a dead end, and a crawler may spend effort following a link that provides no useful page. Site audits identify these errors so important navigation paths can be repaired.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":77,"term":"Crawlability","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":22,"definition":"Whether the crawler can reach and read your pages. Nothing you write matters if the crawler cannot get to it.","explanation":"Crawlability asks whether a search-engine robot can access and read the relevant pages. Good text is not enough if important content is blocked or unreachable. The course treats crawlability as a foundation for subsequent indexing and ranking.","example":"If a crawler cannot reach a page, it cannot reliably inspect that page’s content for indexing. A site may therefore need technical fixes before content improvements can make a difference in search.","full":"Whether the crawler can reach and read your pages. Nothing you write matters if the crawler cannot get to it.\n\nCrawlability asks whether a search-engine robot can access and read the relevant pages. Good text is not enough if important content is blocked or unreachable. The course treats crawlability as a foundation for subsequent indexing and ranking.\n\nIf a crawler cannot reach a page, it cannot reliably inspect that page’s content for indexing. A site may therefore need technical fixes before content improvements can make a difference in search.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":78,"term":"Site Health score","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":22,"definition":"The overall score a site audit gives your domain, alongside a ranked list of errors and warnings. A site audit turns technical SEO into a to-do list.","explanation":"Site-audit tools combine findings into a general health score while also listing errors and warnings. The score gives an overview, but the actionable value lies in the specific problems identified. Fixes should be prioritised by their significance rather than treating the score as the whole objective.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"The overall score a site audit gives your domain, alongside a ranked list of errors and warnings. A site audit turns technical SEO into a to-do list.\n\nSite-audit tools combine findings into a general health score while also listing errors and warnings. The score gives an overview, but the actionable value lies in the specific problems identified. Fixes should be prioritised by their significance rather than treating the score as the whole objective.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":79,"term":"Markup (structured data)","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":22,"definition":"Code that tells robots about the content on your site, so that it can be understood and displayed correctly in search results.","explanation":"Structured data labels information in a form that machines can interpret more precisely. It helps search systems understand what a page contains and may support particular ways information is displayed. It is different from the visible copy, although it should describe that content accurately.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"Code that tells robots about the content on your site, so that it can be understood and displayed correctly in search results.\n\nStructured data labels information in a form that machines can interpret more precisely. It helps search systems understand what a page contains and may support particular ways information is displayed. It is different from the visible copy, although it should describe that content accurately.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TECHNICAL SEO"},{"id":80,"term":"Semrush","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":22,"definition":"The tool used in the assignment: site audit, keywords and backlinks. Open a demo account, find Site Audit, enter your domain and start the crawl.","explanation":"The course uses Semrush for investigating a site’s technical condition, keywords and backlinks. A site audit begins by entering the relevant domain and letting the tool crawl it. The output provides findings to interpret and prioritise rather than replacing human judgement.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"The tool used in the assignment: site audit, keywords and backlinks. Open a demo account, find Site Audit, enter your domain and start the crawl.\n\nThe course uses Semrush for investigating a site’s technical condition, keywords and backlinks. A site audit begins by entering the relevant domain and letting the tool crawl it. The output provides findings to interpret and prioritise rather than replacing human judgement.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TOOLS"},{"id":81,"term":"PageSpeed Insights","lecture":"Lecture 4 · On-site, off-site and technical SEO","page":22,"definition":"pagespeed.web.dev — a free tool that reports Core Web Vitals and performance for a single page.","explanation":"This free Google tool analyses performance for an individual page at pagespeed.web.dev. It reports page-experience information including Core Web Vitals. It can be used to find where page speed or responsiveness needs attention.","example":"This matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","full":"pagespeed.web.dev — a free tool that reports Core Web Vitals and performance for a single page.\n\nThis free Google tool analyses performance for an individual page at pagespeed.web.dev. It reports page-experience information including Core Web Vitals. It can be used to find where page speed or responsiveness needs attention.\n\nThis matters during a site audit because the issue should be connected to a concrete consequence for visitors, crawlers or how a page is understood, rather than simply listed as a technical label.","category":"TOOLS"},{"id":82,"term":"Prompt intent","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":24,"definition":"The job behind a prompt to an answer engine. The same informational, navigational, commercial and transactional intents transfer from search, but prompts can express more context and combine several intents.","explanation":"A prompt to an answer engine still comes from a person with a job to accomplish. Unlike a short search query, it may include detailed circumstances or several linked needs in the same request. Marketers need to understand the underlying purpose rather than treating prompt words as isolated keywords.","example":"A prompt can combine several questions, such as asking for a comparison and an explanation of eligibility at the same time. Understanding the underlying job helps determine which information a generated answer must address.","full":"The job behind a prompt to an answer engine. The same informational, navigational, commercial and transactional intents transfer from search, but prompts can express more context and combine several intents.\n\nA prompt to an answer engine still comes from a person with a job to accomplish. Unlike a short search query, it may include detailed circumstances or several linked needs in the same request. Marketers need to understand the underlying purpose rather than treating prompt words as isolated keywords.\n\nA prompt can combine several questions, such as asking for a comparison and an explanation of eligibility at the same time. Understanding the underlying job helps determine which information a generated answer must address.","category":"INTENT"},{"id":83,"term":"GEO (Generative Engine Optimisation)","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":24,"definition":"The work of making content clear, consistent and structured enough to be quoted and cited in generated answers. GEO extends SEO; it does not replace it.","explanation":"GEO aims to help answer engines understand, summarise and cite information reliably. Clear answers, consistent facts and organised content make a source easier to use in generated responses. The course explicitly says GEO extends SEO rather than replacing the work required for normal search visibility.","example":"For example, a page with a direct answer, consistent facts and a clearly identified organisation is easier for an answer system to interpret and potentially cite. The lecture explicitly frames GEO as an extension of SEO, not a replacement.","full":"The work of making content clear, consistent and structured enough to be quoted and cited in generated answers. GEO extends SEO; it does not replace it.\n\nGEO aims to help answer engines understand, summarise and cite information reliably. Clear answers, consistent facts and organised content make a source easier to use in generated responses. The course explicitly says GEO extends SEO rather than replacing the work required for normal search visibility.\n\nFor example, a page with a direct answer, consistent facts and a clearly identified organisation is easier for an answer system to interpret and potentially cite. The lecture explicitly frames GEO as an extension of SEO, not a replacement.","category":"GEO"},{"id":84,"term":"Answer block","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":24,"definition":"A concise two-sentence answer near the top of a page that resolves its main job before presenting the detail.","explanation":"An answer block resolves the main question near the beginning of a page, usually in about two sentences. Supporting details can then follow below. The structure helps people and answer engines identify the central answer without having to extract it from a long introduction.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"A concise two-sentence answer near the top of a page that resolves its main job before presenting the detail.\n\nAn answer block resolves the main question near the beginning of a page, usually in about two sentences. Supporting details can then follow below. The structure helps people and answer engines identify the central answer without having to extract it from a long introduction.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"GEO"},{"id":85,"term":"Canonical tag","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":24,"definition":"An HTML signal used to identify the preferred URL when the same or very similar content exists on multiple URLs. It helps search engines understand which version should be treated as the main one.","explanation":"When multiple URLs display the same or very similar information, the canonical signal identifies the preferred version. This helps search systems distinguish the main page from duplicates. The chosen URL should provide a consistent reference for that content.","example":"If similar content is accessible at several URLs, a canonical signal helps identify which version should be treated as the main one. This reduces ambiguity about which page represents the content.","full":"An HTML signal used to identify the preferred URL when the same or very similar content exists on multiple URLs. It helps search engines understand which version should be treated as the main one.\n\nWhen multiple URLs display the same or very similar information, the canonical signal identifies the preferred version. This helps search systems distinguish the main page from duplicates. The chosen URL should provide a consistent reference for that content.\n\nIf similar content is accessible at several URLs, a canonical signal helps identify which version should be treated as the main one. This reduces ambiguity about which page represents the content.","category":"GEO"},{"id":86,"term":"Entity","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":25,"definition":"A clearly identified thing — such as an organisation, programme, service or location — with one consistent name, set of facts and canonical page.","explanation":"An entity is a specific identifiable thing rather than a vague keyword, such as a programme or organisation. Using one consistent name and set of facts makes it easier to recognise across pages and answers. The course also links that consistency to a clear canonical page.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"A clearly identified thing — such as an organisation, programme, service or location — with one consistent name, set of facts and canonical page.\n\nAn entity is a specific identifiable thing rather than a vague keyword, such as a programme or organisation. Using one consistent name and set of facts makes it easier to recognise across pages and answers. The course also links that consistency to a clear canonical page.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"GEO"},{"id":87,"term":"Keyword analysis","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":25,"definition":"The process of identifying how an audience phrases demand, understanding the intent and value behind those queries, and mapping them to useful pages.","explanation":"Keyword analysis starts with the language people use when looking for something. It considers the intention and value of a query, not just how frequently the words appear. The resulting groups of keywords can be matched to pages that actually answer the need.","example":"For example, related searches may refer to the same need using different wording. Analysis helps decide whether one page can answer them together or whether they require different pages because the intents differ.","full":"The process of identifying how an audience phrases demand, understanding the intent and value behind those queries, and mapping them to useful pages.\n\nKeyword analysis starts with the language people use when looking for something. It considers the intention and value of a query, not just how frequently the words appear. The resulting groups of keywords can be matched to pages that actually answer the need.\n\nFor example, related searches may refer to the same need using different wording. Analysis helps decide whether one page can answer them together or whether they require different pages because the intents differ.","category":"KEYWORD ANALYSIS"},{"id":88,"term":"Seed keyword","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":25,"definition":"A starting phrase used to generate related keyword ideas. Audience, job, intent and the four Ps can all provide seeds.","explanation":"A seed is an initial phrase from which related searches can be explored. Seeds can come from the audience, the job they want done, their intent or the marketing mix. It is a starting point for investigation, not automatically the best final target.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"A starting phrase used to generate related keyword ideas. Audience, job, intent and the four Ps can all provide seeds.\n\nA seed is an initial phrase from which related searches can be explored. Seeds can come from the audience, the job they want done, their intent or the marketing mix. It is a starting point for investigation, not automatically the best final target.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"KEYWORD ANALYSIS"},{"id":89,"term":"Search volume","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":25,"definition":"The estimated number of searches for a query in a defined place and time period. It shows frequency, not the value or relevance of the query.","explanation":"Volume estimates how often a query is searched within a particular location and time. A large number indicates frequency, not whether the visitors are relevant or likely to act. It therefore needs to be interpreted together with intent and the page’s purpose.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"The estimated number of searches for a query in a defined place and time period. It shows frequency, not the value or relevance of the query.\n\nVolume estimates how often a query is searched within a particular location and time. A large number indicates frequency, not whether the visitors are relevant or likely to act. It therefore needs to be interpreted together with intent and the page’s purpose.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"KEYWORD ANALYSIS"},{"id":90,"term":"Keyword difficulty","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":26,"definition":"An estimate of how difficult it may be to rank organically for a keyword, based on the strength and competition of the existing results.","explanation":"Difficulty estimates the competition facing a page that wants to rank for a query. It helps compare opportunities but is not the same as customer value or intent. A highly relevant lower-volume query may be more useful than a popular one that is hard to rank for.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"An estimate of how difficult it may be to rank organically for a keyword, based on the strength and competition of the existing results.\n\nDifficulty estimates the competition facing a page that wants to rank for a query. It helps compare opportunities but is not the same as customer value or intent. A highly relevant lower-volume query may be more useful than a popular one that is hard to rank for.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"KEYWORD ANALYSIS"},{"id":91,"term":"Keyword cluster","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":26,"definition":"A group of related queries that share a topic and intent and can usually be answered by the same canonical page.","explanation":"A cluster combines related search phrases that share a subject and similar intent. One useful canonical page can often answer several such phrases. This avoids creating a separate near-duplicate page for every small variation.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"A group of related queries that share a topic and intent and can usually be answered by the same canonical page.\n\nA cluster combines related search phrases that share a subject and similar intent. One useful canonical page can often answer several such phrases. This avoids creating a separate near-duplicate page for every small variation.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"KEYWORD ANALYSIS"},{"id":92,"term":"Primary keyword","lecture":"Lecture 5 · GEO and keyword analysis","page":26,"definition":"The main query a page is designed to answer. Related phrases support it; a different intent normally requires a different page.","explanation":"The primary keyword expresses the main search need that a particular page should answer. Supporting phrases can add context without replacing that main purpose. A substantially different search intent normally calls for a different page.","example":"The lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","full":"The main query a page is designed to answer. Related phrases support it; a different intent normally requires a different page.\n\nThe primary keyword expresses the main search need that a particular page should answer. Supporting phrases can add context without replacing that main purpose. A substantially different search intent normally calls for a different page.\n\nThe lecture connects this decision to the audience’s actual question and to providing a clear, useful answer, not simply to including a phrase as often as possible.","category":"KEYWORD ANALYSIS"},{"id":93,"term":"Cognitive Load Theory","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":27,"definition":"A theory about how people process new information while thinking and learning, centred on the limited capacity of working memory.","explanation":"The theory explains why presenting too much unfamiliar information at once can overwhelm a person. Working memory has limited capacity, while organised knowledge in long-term memory can make processing easier. In communication design, clarity matters because presentation choices can create or reduce unnecessary mental effort.","example":"If an advertisement contains too many unfamiliar ideas and competing messages, a viewer may struggle to process it. The lecture connects the limited capacity of working memory with the importance of communicating clearly.","full":"A theory about how people process new information while thinking and learning, centred on the limited capacity of working memory.\n\nThe theory explains why presenting too much unfamiliar information at once can overwhelm a person. Working memory has limited capacity, while organised knowledge in long-term memory can make processing easier. In communication design, clarity matters because presentation choices can create or reduce unnecessary mental effort.\n\nIf an advertisement contains too many unfamiliar ideas and competing messages, a viewer may struggle to process it. The lecture connects the limited capacity of working memory with the importance of communicating clearly.","category":"COGNITIVE LOAD THEORY"},{"id":94,"term":"Working memory","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":27,"definition":"The memory system where we consciously process information. It can handle only a limited amount of unfamiliar information at one time.","explanation":"Working memory is used while a person is actively reading, comparing or understanding new information. Because its capacity is limited, too many unfamiliar elements can make a message difficult to follow. The course uses this to explain why marketing communication should avoid unnecessary complexity.","example":"For example, a person comparing several unfamiliar prices, features and instructions at once may lose track of some details. The limited capacity of working memory explains why cluttered communication is difficult to follow.","full":"The memory system where we consciously process information. It can handle only a limited amount of unfamiliar information at one time.\n\nWorking memory is used while a person is actively reading, comparing or understanding new information. Because its capacity is limited, too many unfamiliar elements can make a message difficult to follow. The course uses this to explain why marketing communication should avoid unnecessary complexity.\n\nFor example, a person comparing several unfamiliar prices, features and instructions at once may lose track of some details. The limited capacity of working memory explains why cluttered communication is difficult to follow.","category":"MEMORY SYSTEMS"},{"id":95,"term":"Long-term memory","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":27,"definition":"The memory system that stores organised knowledge for later use. It is not limited in the same way as working memory.","explanation":"Long-term memory holds knowledge that has already been learned and organised. A familiar idea can be processed more easily because the person can draw on existing knowledge. This helps explain why recognition and prior experience affect how demanding new information feels.","example":"Someone who already understands a topic may recognise an idea as part of a familiar pattern rather than having to process every element from the beginning. This helps explain why existing knowledge changes how difficult new material feels.","full":"The memory system that stores organised knowledge for later use. It is not limited in the same way as working memory.\n\nLong-term memory holds knowledge that has already been learned and organised. A familiar idea can be processed more easily because the person can draw on existing knowledge. This helps explain why recognition and prior experience affect how demanding new information feels.\n\nSomeone who already understands a topic may recognise an idea as part of a familiar pattern rather than having to process every element from the beginning. This helps explain why existing knowledge changes how difficult new material feels.","category":"MEMORY SYSTEMS"},{"id":96,"term":"Schema","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":27,"definition":"An organised structure that connects several pieces of knowledge, making information easier to process and retrieve.","explanation":"A schema organises related knowledge into a meaningful structure. Instead of having to process each detail separately, a person can use that structure to understand a larger idea. Building and using schemas therefore helps reduce the effort required by unfamiliar material.","example":"For example, knowledge of how online checkout normally works creates a familiar structure for interpreting buttons, prices and payment steps on a new site. Related pieces of information can be processed as a connected whole.","full":"An organised structure that connects several pieces of knowledge, making information easier to process and retrieve.\n\nA schema organises related knowledge into a meaningful structure. Instead of having to process each detail separately, a person can use that structure to understand a larger idea. Building and using schemas therefore helps reduce the effort required by unfamiliar material.\n\nFor example, knowledge of how online checkout normally works creates a familiar structure for interpreting buttons, prices and payment steps on a new site. Related pieces of information can be processed as a connected whole.","category":"MEMORY SYSTEMS"},{"id":97,"term":"Cognitive load","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":28,"definition":"The demands placed on working memory while a person processes information.","explanation":"Cognitive load is the amount of mental effort a task demands from working memory. Some load comes from the task itself, while some is added unnecessarily by poor presentation. Designers can often improve understanding by reducing the avoidable part.","example":"In communication design, the practical question is whether people have enough capacity to understand the essential message without spending effort on avoidable presentation problems.","full":"The demands placed on working memory while a person processes information.\n\nCognitive load is the amount of mental effort a task demands from working memory. Some load comes from the task itself, while some is added unnecessarily by poor presentation. Designers can often improve understanding by reducing the avoidable part.\n\nIn communication design, the practical question is whether people have enough capacity to understand the essential message without spending effort on avoidable presentation problems.","category":"COGNITIVE LOAD THEORY"},{"id":98,"term":"Extraneous cognitive load","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":28,"definition":"Unnecessary processing created by how information is presented or by the design of a task.","explanation":"This is effort caused by the way information is delivered rather than by the important meaning of the information. Examples in the course include splitting related information across locations, repeating the same message without benefit and making information disappear too quickly. Better design reduces this unnecessary burden.","example":"For example, placing an explanation far from the image it describes forces viewers to search back and forth. That effort is produced by presentation, not by the underlying subject.","full":"Unnecessary processing created by how information is presented or by the design of a task.\n\nThis is effort caused by the way information is delivered rather than by the important meaning of the information. Examples in the course include splitting related information across locations, repeating the same message without benefit and making information disappear too quickly. Better design reduces this unnecessary burden.\n\nFor example, placing an explanation far from the image it describes forces viewers to search back and forth. That effort is produced by presentation, not by the underlying subject.","category":"COGNITIVE LOAD THEORY"},{"id":99,"term":"Intrinsic cognitive load","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":28,"definition":"The necessary complexity of the information or task. It depends on how many elements must be understood together and what the person already knows.","explanation":"Intrinsic load comes from the genuine difficulty of learning or processing the subject. A task involving many connected unfamiliar ideas demands more than a simple familiar task. Unlike extraneous load, it cannot simply be removed through cosmetic presentation changes.","example":"Learning several interacting concepts is harder than learning one isolated label. This complexity belongs to the task itself; it cannot simply be removed by changing fonts or colours.","full":"The necessary complexity of the information or task. It depends on how many elements must be understood together and what the person already knows.\n\nIntrinsic load comes from the genuine difficulty of learning or processing the subject. A task involving many connected unfamiliar ideas demands more than a simple familiar task. Unlike extraneous load, it cannot simply be removed through cosmetic presentation changes.\n\nLearning several interacting concepts is harder than learning one isolated label. This complexity belongs to the task itself; it cannot simply be removed by changing fonts or colours.","category":"COGNITIVE LOAD THEORY"},{"id":100,"term":"Split-attention effect","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":28,"definition":"When related information is separated and the audience must use working memory to connect it. Placing related information together reduces this load.","explanation":"If the caption describing a diagram is far from the relevant part of the diagram, the reader must keep switching between them. This uses working memory for matching rather than understanding. The course recommends keeping related information close together.","example":"A diagram on one page and its explanation on another require the reader to hold one part in memory while searching for the other. Placing them together reduces that unnecessary effort.","full":"When related information is separated and the audience must use working memory to connect it. Placing related information together reduces this load.\n\nIf the caption describing a diagram is far from the relevant part of the diagram, the reader must keep switching between them. This uses working memory for matching rather than understanding. The course recommends keeping related information close together.\n\nA diagram on one page and its explanation on another require the reader to hold one part in memory while searching for the other. Placing them together reduces that unnecessary effort.","category":"EXTRANEOUS LOAD"},{"id":101,"term":"Redundancy effect","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":29,"definition":"Unnecessary processing caused by repeating the same message without adding understanding. Text and visuals should have complementary jobs.","explanation":"Repeated text and visuals can create extra processing when they merely say the same thing. Communication becomes clearer when each element adds a useful contribution. The aim is not to eliminate explanation, but to avoid duplication that does not improve comprehension.","example":"Repeating identical words on a slide and speaking the same text does not automatically improve understanding. The course recommends letting visual and written elements serve complementary purposes.","full":"Unnecessary processing caused by repeating the same message without adding understanding. Text and visuals should have complementary jobs.\n\nRepeated text and visuals can create extra processing when they merely say the same thing. Communication becomes clearer when each element adds a useful contribution. The aim is not to eliminate explanation, but to avoid duplication that does not improve comprehension.\n\nRepeating identical words on a slide and speaking the same text does not automatically improve understanding. The course recommends letting visual and written elements serve complementary purposes.","category":"EXTRANEOUS LOAD"},{"id":102,"term":"Transient information effect","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":29,"definition":"The load created when information disappears before it is understood, forcing the audience to hold it in working memory.","explanation":"A temporary message, slide or moving element may disappear before the audience has understood it. The user then has to hold the unfinished information in working memory. Giving people enough time or making important material available reduces this problem.","example":"A brief spoken instruction or disappearing screen may vanish before someone processes it. Keeping essential information available longer can reduce the need to remember it while learning the next point.","full":"The load created when information disappears before it is understood, forcing the audience to hold it in working memory.\n\nA temporary message, slide or moving element may disappear before the audience has understood it. The user then has to hold the unfinished information in working memory. Giving people enough time or making important material available reduces this problem.\n\nA brief spoken instruction or disappearing screen may vanish before someone processes it. Keeping essential information available longer can reduce the need to remember it while learning the next point.","category":"EXTRANEOUS LOAD"},{"id":103,"term":"Walled garden","lecture":"Lecture 6 · Cognitive load theory and outbound marketing","page":29,"definition":"A closed digital ecosystem where one company controls the content, user data, ad inventory and measurement.","explanation":"A walled garden keeps audiences, content, advertising opportunities and measurement within one company’s environment. Businesses can reach people inside that platform, but they depend on the platform’s rules and data. This illustrates the control major intermediaries have over digital advertising.","example":"In communication design, the practical question is whether people have enough capacity to understand the essential message without spending effort on avoidable presentation problems.","full":"A closed digital ecosystem where one company controls the content, user data, ad inventory and measurement.\n\nA walled garden keeps audiences, content, advertising opportunities and measurement within one company’s environment. Businesses can reach people inside that platform, but they depend on the platform’s rules and data. This illustrates the control major intermediaries have over digital advertising.\n\nIn communication design, the practical question is whether people have enough capacity to understand the essential message without spending effort on avoidable presentation problems.","category":"OUTBOUND MARKETING"},{"id":104,"term":"SEM (Search Engine Marketing)","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":30,"definition":"Paid advertisements that appear in search-engine results, targeting specific keywords to reach people actively searching for related products or services.","explanation":"The course defines SEM in terms of paid ads shown in search results. Advertisers choose keywords connected to what people are actively searching for. The query signals a need, while the advertisement and its landing page should respond to that need.","example":"For example, a paid search ad can appear when someone types a relevant keyword. The lecture connects this decision with the advertisement itself, the landing page and the campaign objective.","full":"Paid advertisements that appear in search-engine results, targeting specific keywords to reach people actively searching for related products or services.\n\nThe course defines SEM in terms of paid ads shown in search results. Advertisers choose keywords connected to what people are actively searching for. The query signals a need, while the advertisement and its landing page should respond to that need.\n\nFor example, a paid search ad can appear when someone types a relevant keyword. The lecture connects this decision with the advertisement itself, the landing page and the campaign objective.","category":"SEM"},{"id":105,"term":"Distinctiveness","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":30,"definition":"Being easy to notice and recognise as your brand through consistent distinctive assets such as colours, shapes, taglines, characters, sonic cues and pack shapes.","explanation":"A distinctive brand is easy to notice and recognise because it repeatedly uses recognisable cues. The course names colours, shapes, characters, taglines, sounds and packaging as possible assets. Distinctiveness helps someone connect a message to the correct brand.","example":"A consistent colour, sound or character can help people identify a brand quickly. It is not simply about looking unusual: the cue needs to be linked reliably to the brand in the audience’s memory.","full":"Being easy to notice and recognise as your brand through consistent distinctive assets such as colours, shapes, taglines, characters, sonic cues and pack shapes.\n\nA distinctive brand is easy to notice and recognise because it repeatedly uses recognisable cues. The course names colours, shapes, characters, taglines, sounds and packaging as possible assets. Distinctiveness helps someone connect a message to the correct brand.\n\nA consistent colour, sound or character can help people identify a brand quickly. It is not simply about looking unusual: the cue needs to be linked reliably to the brand in the audience’s memory.","category":"DISTINCTION AND CREATIVITY"},{"id":106,"term":"Distinctive brand asset","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":30,"definition":"A recognisable cue linked to a brand, such as a colour, shape, tagline, character, sound, promise or pack shape.","explanation":"This is a particular feature people associate with a brand, such as its familiar colour or sound. Repeated consistent use helps strengthen recognition. An asset is useful when it helps identify the brand, rather than merely decorating an advertisement.","example":"The relevant marketing question is how this affects what people notice, remember or choose, not whether the technique can be used without considering the audience.","full":"A recognisable cue linked to a brand, such as a colour, shape, tagline, character, sound, promise or pack shape.\n\nThis is a particular feature people associate with a brand, such as its familiar colour or sound. Repeated consistent use helps strengthen recognition. An asset is useful when it helps identify the brand, rather than merely decorating an advertisement.\n\nThe relevant marketing question is how this affects what people notice, remember or choose, not whether the technique can be used without considering the audience.","category":"DISTINCTION AND CREATIVITY"},{"id":107,"term":"Brand image","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":30,"definition":"How a brand is being perceived.","explanation":"Brand image concerns the perceptions people actually hold about an organisation. It may not match what the organisation intends to communicate. Understanding the distinction matters because the audience, not the company, determines how the brand is perceived.","example":"Even if a company wants to appear innovative, customers may perceive it as conventional. That gap is the difference between the intended identity and the actual image held by the audience.","full":"How a brand is being perceived.\n\nBrand image concerns the perceptions people actually hold about an organisation. It may not match what the organisation intends to communicate. Understanding the distinction matters because the audience, not the company, determines how the brand is perceived.\n\nEven if a company wants to appear innovative, customers may perceive it as conventional. That gap is the difference between the intended identity and the actual image held by the audience.","category":"DISTINCTION AND CREATIVITY"},{"id":108,"term":"Brand identity","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":31,"definition":"How a brand wants to be perceived.","explanation":"Brand identity is the way a brand deliberately presents itself and wishes to be understood. It may involve its chosen visual and verbal cues. The difference from brand image is intention versus actual audience perception.","example":"A company may intend to be seen as dependable or creative. That intended identity is not automatically the same as its brand image, which depends on how other people actually perceive it.","full":"How a brand wants to be perceived.\n\nBrand identity is the way a brand deliberately presents itself and wishes to be understood. It may involve its chosen visual and verbal cues. The difference from brand image is intention versus actual audience perception.\n\nA company may intend to be seen as dependable or creative. That intended identity is not automatically the same as its brand image, which depends on how other people actually perceive it.","category":"DISTINCTION AND CREATIVITY"},{"id":109,"term":"CPM (cost per mille)","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":31,"definition":"An advertising billing model in which the advertiser pays per 1,000 impressions.","explanation":"Under CPM pricing, the advertiser pays for every 1,000 counted impressions. The cost is connected to display opportunities rather than clicks or purchases. It is therefore important not to confuse the volume of impressions with proof of attention or sales.","example":"If the rate is 50 currency units per thousand impressions, buying 10,000 impressions would cost 500 units. The payment is linked to displayed impressions, not necessarily to attention or purchases.","full":"An advertising billing model in which the advertiser pays per 1,000 impressions.\n\nUnder CPM pricing, the advertiser pays for every 1,000 counted impressions. The cost is connected to display opportunities rather than clicks or purchases. It is therefore important not to confuse the volume of impressions with proof of attention or sales.\n\nIf the rate is 50 currency units per thousand impressions, buying 10,000 impressions would cost 500 units. The payment is linked to displayed impressions, not necessarily to attention or purchases.","category":"CAMPAIGN MEASUREMENT"},{"id":110,"term":"CPC (cost per click)","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":31,"definition":"An advertising billing model in which the advertiser pays for each valid click.","explanation":"Under CPC pricing, cost is linked to valid clicks on an advertisement. This makes a click the billing event, but it does not guarantee that the visitor buys something. The lecture’s later discussion of advertising measurement warns against treating clicks as proof of business effect.","example":"If an advertiser pays 5 currency units per valid click and receives 100 billed clicks, the cost is 500 units. The billing model says what triggers payment, not whether the clicks lead to useful outcomes.","full":"An advertising billing model in which the advertiser pays for each valid click.\n\nUnder CPC pricing, cost is linked to valid clicks on an advertisement. This makes a click the billing event, but it does not guarantee that the visitor buys something. The lecture’s later discussion of advertising measurement warns against treating clicks as proof of business effect.\n\nIf an advertiser pays 5 currency units per valid click and receives 100 billed clicks, the cost is 500 units. The billing model says what triggers payment, not whether the clicks lead to useful outcomes.","category":"CAMPAIGN MEASUREMENT"},{"id":111,"term":"Anchoring effect","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":31,"definition":"A cognitive bias where people rely heavily on the first piece of information they encounter when making decisions.","explanation":"The first number or fact encountered can shape how later options are judged. For example, an initial high price can become a reference point against which another price seems low. The important mechanism is reliance on the first piece of information, not a full independent comparison.","example":"For example, showing a high initial price before a lower one can make the second price appear especially attractive. The first number shapes the reference point for judgment.","full":"A cognitive bias where people rely heavily on the first piece of information they encounter when making decisions.\n\nThe first number or fact encountered can shape how later options are judged. For example, an initial high price can become a reference point against which another price seems low. The important mechanism is reliance on the first piece of information, not a full independent comparison.\n\nFor example, showing a high initial price before a lower one can make the second price appear especially attractive. The first number shapes the reference point for judgment.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":112,"term":"Scarcity","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":31,"definition":"A principle where limited availability is perceived as more valuable, prompting faster decisions. Scarcity can concern quantity, time, demand or access.","explanation":"People may assign greater value or urgency to something that appears limited. The limit can concern time, quantity, demand or access. This can influence decisions by making delay feel costly.","example":"For instance, a genuinely limited number of available places can make delaying a decision feel costly. The lecture distinguishes limitations of quantity, time, demand and access.","full":"A principle where limited availability is perceived as more valuable, prompting faster decisions. Scarcity can concern quantity, time, demand or access.\n\nPeople may assign greater value or urgency to something that appears limited. The limit can concern time, quantity, demand or access. This can influence decisions by making delay feel costly.\n\nFor instance, a genuinely limited number of available places can make delaying a decision feel costly. The lecture distinguishes limitations of quantity, time, demand and access.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":113,"term":"Loss aversion","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":32,"definition":"The principle that people are more motivated to avoid losses than to acquire equivalent gains.","explanation":"A potential loss can have a stronger motivational effect than an equivalent possible gain. A message that emphasises what someone might lose may therefore feel different from one describing what they could gain. The principle concerns how choices are perceived, not an objective change in value.","example":"For instance, a message about losing an existing benefit may affect decisions differently from a message offering an equivalent gain. The framing of the possible loss matters.","full":"The principle that people are more motivated to avoid losses than to acquire equivalent gains.\n\nA potential loss can have a stronger motivational effect than an equivalent possible gain. A message that emphasises what someone might lose may therefore feel different from one describing what they could gain. The principle concerns how choices are perceived, not an objective change in value.\n\nFor instance, a message about losing an existing benefit may affect decisions differently from a message offering an equivalent gain. The framing of the possible loss matters.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":114,"term":"Reciprocity","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":32,"definition":"The principle that people feel compelled to return a favour or gesture after receiving something of value.","explanation":"Receiving something useful or generous can create a feeling that one should respond in kind. In marketing, the idea helps explain why offering value may influence later behaviour. The response is based on a perceived social obligation, not simply a direct transaction.","example":"For example, someone who receives a genuinely useful resource may feel more willing to respond positively to the organisation that provided it. The effect depends on the perceived value of the initial gesture.","full":"The principle that people feel compelled to return a favour or gesture after receiving something of value.\n\nReceiving something useful or generous can create a feeling that one should respond in kind. In marketing, the idea helps explain why offering value may influence later behaviour. The response is based on a perceived social obligation, not simply a direct transaction.\n\nFor example, someone who receives a genuinely useful resource may feel more willing to respond positively to the organisation that provided it. The effect depends on the perceived value of the initial gesture.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":115,"term":"Priming","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":32,"definition":"Prior exposure to a cue that changes how a person perceives, interprets or responds to related information.","explanation":"A cue encountered earlier can affect the interpretation of something that appears later. The influence may occur even when the person is not consciously thinking about the first cue. The principle helps explain how context changes perception.","example":"The relevant marketing question is how this affects what people notice, remember or choose, not whether the technique can be used without considering the audience.","full":"Prior exposure to a cue that changes how a person perceives, interprets or responds to related information.\n\nA cue encountered earlier can affect the interpretation of something that appears later. The influence may occur even when the person is not consciously thinking about the first cue. The principle helps explain how context changes perception.\n\nThe relevant marketing question is how this affects what people notice, remember or choose, not whether the technique can be used without considering the audience.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":116,"term":"Framing","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":32,"definition":"Presenting information in a way that emphasises certain aspects and influences how people perceive and react to it.","explanation":"The facts can remain the same while the way they are presented changes the audience’s reaction. Highlighting a gain, loss, risk or benefit gives one interpretation greater prominence. Framing is therefore about presentation and perception rather than changing the underlying information.","example":"Describing the same offer through a possible gain rather than a possible loss can change how it is interpreted. The underlying facts may remain the same while the emphasis changes.","full":"Presenting information in a way that emphasises certain aspects and influences how people perceive and react to it.\n\nThe facts can remain the same while the way they are presented changes the audience’s reaction. Highlighting a gain, loss, risk or benefit gives one interpretation greater prominence. Framing is therefore about presentation and perception rather than changing the underlying information.\n\nDescribing the same offer through a possible gain rather than a possible loss can change how it is interpreted. The underlying facts may remain the same while the emphasis changes.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":117,"term":"Trust signals","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":33,"definition":"Visual or textual cues that reassure people about a brand's credibility, reliability and security, such as reviews, security badges or guarantees.","explanation":"Signals such as reviews, guarantees and security badges can reduce uncertainty about a purchase or service. They provide cues about reliability and credibility. Their usefulness depends on whether the cues are meaningful and trustworthy.","example":"The relevant marketing question is how this affects what people notice, remember or choose, not whether the technique can be used without considering the audience.","full":"Visual or textual cues that reassure people about a brand's credibility, reliability and security, such as reviews, security badges or guarantees.\n\nSignals such as reviews, guarantees and security badges can reduce uncertainty about a purchase or service. They provide cues about reliability and credibility. Their usefulness depends on whether the cues are meaningful and trustworthy.\n\nThe relevant marketing question is how this affects what people notice, remember or choose, not whether the technique can be used without considering the audience.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":118,"term":"Decoy effect","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":33,"definition":"When a third, less attractive option changes how two other options are compared, making one appear more appealing.","explanation":"An additional option can change the relative attractiveness of two existing choices. The third option is designed or positioned so that one of the original options appears preferable. This shows that decisions depend on the comparison set, not only on each option considered alone.","example":"A third pricing option can make one of two existing options seem comparatively better, even if the third option attracts few buyers itself. The decision changes because the comparison changes.","full":"When a third, less attractive option changes how two other options are compared, making one appear more appealing.\n\nAn additional option can change the relative attractiveness of two existing choices. The third option is designed or positioned so that one of the original options appears preferable. This shows that decisions depend on the comparison set, not only on each option considered alone.\n\nA third pricing option can make one of two existing options seem comparatively better, even if the third option attracts few buyers itself. The decision changes because the comparison changes.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":119,"term":"Peak-end rule","lecture":"Lecture 7 · SEM, distinction and decision-making","page":33,"definition":"The principle that people judge an experience largely by its most intense moment and its end, rather than by the whole experience.","explanation":"A person’s memory of an experience is especially influenced by its emotional high point and its ending. The experience may therefore be judged differently from a simple average of every moment. This principle is relevant when thinking about how a customer remembers an interaction.","example":"For instance, a customer might remember a particularly frustrating support interaction and the way it was resolved more strongly than every routine step in between. Those moments influence the overall evaluation.","full":"The principle that people judge an experience largely by its most intense moment and its end, rather than by the whole experience.\n\nA person’s memory of an experience is especially influenced by its emotional high point and its ending. The experience may therefore be judged differently from a simple average of every moment. This principle is relevant when thinking about how a customer remembers an interaction.\n\nFor instance, a customer might remember a particularly frustrating support interaction and the way it was resolved more strongly than every routine step in between. Those moments influence the overall evaluation.","category":"DECISION-MAKING"},{"id":120,"term":"Assistant-centric web","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":34,"definition":"A web where people increasingly receive a composed answer from an assistant instead of searching through and choosing between a list of webpages.","explanation":"The user can ask a question and receive a composed response instead of selecting and reading several search results. The intermediary assistant affects which information is surfaced and how it is expressed. This has consequences for organisations whose content previously depended on direct website visits.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"A web where people increasingly receive a composed answer from an assistant instead of searching through and choosing between a list of webpages.\n\nThe user can ask a question and receive a composed response instead of selecting and reading several search results. The intermediary assistant affects which information is surfaced and how it is expressed. This has consequences for organisations whose content previously depended on direct website visits.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"THE DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT"},{"id":121,"term":"Attention economy","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":34,"definition":"An environment where information is abundant, human attention is limited, and access to audiences can therefore be sold.","explanation":"Digital information can be produced and distributed at enormous scale, but people cannot pay attention to everything. Audience attention consequently becomes something platforms and advertisers compete over and sell access to. The course questions whether sold impressions reliably represent meaningful human attention.","example":"Digital services compete for a limited resource: time and human attention. The lecture questions whether the amount of advertising activity sold really corresponds to valuable human attention.","full":"An environment where information is abundant, human attention is limited, and access to audiences can therefore be sold.\n\nDigital information can be produced and distributed at enormous scale, but people cannot pay attention to everything. Audience attention consequently becomes something platforms and advertisers compete over and sell access to. The course questions whether sold impressions reliably represent meaningful human attention.\n\nDigital services compete for a limited resource: time and human attention. The lecture questions whether the amount of advertising activity sold really corresponds to valuable human attention.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":122,"term":"Impression","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":34,"definition":"An opportunity to display an ad that has been counted as served. It is not proof that anyone noticed the ad.","explanation":"An impression is a counted event indicating an advertisement was served. It does not necessarily mean the ad was on screen long enough to be seen, or that a person actually noticed it. This difference matters when evaluating digital advertising reports.","example":"For example, an ad can be counted as served even when the person never notices it. Impression totals should therefore not be mistaken for evidence that an audience actually paid attention.","full":"An opportunity to display an ad that has been counted as served. It is not proof that anyone noticed the ad.\n\nAn impression is a counted event indicating an advertisement was served. It does not necessarily mean the ad was on screen long enough to be seen, or that a person actually noticed it. This difference matters when evaluating digital advertising reports.\n\nFor example, an ad can be counted as served even when the person never notices it. Impression totals should therefore not be mistaken for evidence that an audience actually paid attention.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":123,"term":"Viewability","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":34,"definition":"A measurement of whether enough of an advertisement appeared on screen for long enough to count as viewable. It does not prove that a person noticed it.","explanation":"Viewability checks whether an ad met technical conditions for appearing on screen. It is a stronger claim than simple delivery, but not evidence of genuine attention or persuasion. The course stresses these differences because advertising measurements can be mistaken for outcomes.","example":"A viewability measure checks whether an ad was sufficiently present on screen according to a technical rule. It still cannot reveal whether a human looked at it or cared about it.","full":"A measurement of whether enough of an advertisement appeared on screen for long enough to count as viewable. It does not prove that a person noticed it.\n\nViewability checks whether an ad met technical conditions for appearing on screen. It is a stronger claim than simple delivery, but not evidence of genuine attention or persuasion. The course stresses these differences because advertising measurements can be mistaken for outcomes.\n\nA viewability measure checks whether an ad was sufficiently present on screen according to a technical rule. It still cannot reveal whether a human looked at it or cared about it.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":124,"term":"Attribution","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":35,"definition":"A rule for assigning credit for an outcome, such as a sale, to one or more marketing touchpoints. First click and last click are attribution rules, not proof of effect.","explanation":"An attribution model decides how reported credit is distributed among interactions before an outcome. For example, the first or final click may receive credit for a sale. Such a rule describes measurement, but does not establish that the credited interaction caused the sale.","example":"If a customer clicked an ad before buying, last-click attribution may credit that ad. The lecture stresses that assigning credit through a rule does not establish that the purchase would not have happened otherwise.","full":"A rule for assigning credit for an outcome, such as a sale, to one or more marketing touchpoints. First click and last click are attribution rules, not proof of effect.\n\nAn attribution model decides how reported credit is distributed among interactions before an outcome. For example, the first or final click may receive credit for a sale. Such a rule describes measurement, but does not establish that the credited interaction caused the sale.\n\nIf a customer clicked an ad before buying, last-click attribution may credit that ad. The lecture stresses that assigning credit through a rule does not establish that the purchase would not have happened otherwise.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":125,"term":"Incrementality","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":35,"definition":"An outcome that happened because of the advertising and would not have happened without it.","explanation":"Incrementality asks what extra outcome occurred because the advertising was present. If a customer would have purchased anyway, the ad may be attributed a sale without adding one. The course contrasts incrementality with convenient measures such as clicks and attributed conversions.","example":"If a customer would have bought without seeing the advertisement, the sale is not incremental to that ad. The useful question is what additional outcome happened because the advertising was present.","full":"An outcome that happened because of the advertising and would not have happened without it.\n\nIncrementality asks what extra outcome occurred because the advertising was present. If a customer would have purchased anyway, the ad may be attributed a sale without adding one. The course contrasts incrementality with convenient measures such as clicks and attributed conversions.\n\nIf a customer would have bought without seeing the advertisement, the sale is not incremental to that ad. The useful question is what additional outcome happened because the advertising was present.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":126,"term":"Invalid traffic","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":35,"definition":"Impressions or clicks that do not come from genuine human interest, for example from bots or fraud.","explanation":"Bots, fraudulent clicks and other non-genuine interactions can generate counts without genuine human interest. These events can make campaign numbers appear stronger than the attention actually obtained. This is part of the lecture’s criticism of the digital advertising marketplace.","example":"A bot-generated click may be recorded by a system even though no genuine customer expressed interest. This is one reason the lecture challenges the reliability of reported advertising activity.","full":"Impressions or clicks that do not come from genuine human interest, for example from bots or fraud.\n\nBots, fraudulent clicks and other non-genuine interactions can generate counts without genuine human interest. These events can make campaign numbers appear stronger than the attention actually obtained. This is part of the lecture’s criticism of the digital advertising marketplace.\n\nA bot-generated click may be recorded by a system even though no genuine customer expressed interest. This is one reason the lecture challenges the reliability of reported advertising activity.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":127,"term":"Algorithmic trading of advertising","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":35,"definition":"Software buying and selling ad placements in fractions of a second, making individual transactions difficult for advertisers to inspect.","explanation":"Automated systems buy and sell digital advertising placements extremely quickly. Transactions can be difficult for advertisers to follow or verify individually. The course connects this scale and speed with reduced transparency over what was bought.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Software buying and selling ad placements in fractions of a second, making individual transactions difficult for advertisers to inspect.\n\nAutomated systems buy and sell digital advertising placements extremely quickly. Transactions can be difficult for advertisers to follow or verify individually. The course connects this scale and speed with reduced transparency over what was bought.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":128,"term":"Advertising dark pool","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":35,"definition":"A private marketplace where selected buyers trade ad placements outside the open market, so wider trading activity and prices are not visible.","explanation":"A private marketplace permits selected participants to trade advertising inventory away from the wider open market. Outsiders cannot see all the prices or transactions. This limited visibility makes the true value of the trade harder to assess.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"A private marketplace where selected buyers trade ad placements outside the open market, so wider trading activity and prices are not visible.\n\nA private marketplace permits selected participants to trade advertising inventory away from the wider open market. Outsiders cannot see all the prices or transactions. This limited visibility makes the true value of the trade harder to assess.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":129,"term":"Subprime attention","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":36,"definition":"Hwang's description of attention of uncertain quality being traded as if its value were more reliable: an ad may be unseen, a click manufactured, or a purchase may have happened anyway.","explanation":"Hwang uses this term for uncertain or poor-quality advertising attention sold as if its value were dependable. Some ads are not noticed, some clicks are fake and some credited sales would have happened anyway. The issue is the gap between the reported transaction and real business effect.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Hwang's description of attention of uncertain quality being traded as if its value were more reliable: an ad may be unseen, a click manufactured, or a purchase may have happened anyway.\n\nHwang uses this term for uncertain or poor-quality advertising attention sold as if its value were dependable. Some ads are not noticed, some clicks are fake and some credited sales would have happened anyway. The issue is the gap between the reported transaction and real business effect.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"THE BROKEN MARKETPLACE"},{"id":130,"term":"Good play","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":36,"definition":"Meaningful engagement in digital media combined with responsibility to other people.","explanation":"Good play combines active digital participation with consideration for the effects on other people. It is not enough for an online action to be entertaining or engaging. The ethical dimension asks whether that participation is responsible toward others.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Meaningful engagement in digital media combined with responsibility to other people.\n\nGood play combines active digital participation with consideration for the effects on other people. It is not enough for an online action to be entertaining or engaging. The ethical dimension asks whether that participation is responsible toward others.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":131,"term":"Ethical fault lines","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":36,"definition":"Five areas for analysing ethics online: identity, privacy, ownership and authorship, credibility and participation (James et al., 2009).","explanation":"The course uses five areas from James and colleagues: identity, privacy, ownership and authorship, credibility, and participation. They provide a way to examine different problems rather than reducing all online ethics to one question. More than one area may apply to the same situation.","example":"For example, a public post involving another person’s personal information may raise questions about privacy, identity, credibility and participation at the same time. The five areas help identify the different ethical issues involved.","full":"Five areas for analysing ethics online: identity, privacy, ownership and authorship, credibility and participation (James et al., 2009).\n\nThe course uses five areas from James and colleagues: identity, privacy, ownership and authorship, credibility, and participation. They provide a way to examine different problems rather than reducing all online ethics to one question. More than one area may apply to the same situation.\n\nFor example, a public post involving another person’s personal information may raise questions about privacy, identity, credibility and participation at the same time. The five areas help identify the different ethical issues involved.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":132,"term":"Ethical analysis","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":36,"definition":"A method that describes what happened, identifies who is affected, selects relevant ethical areas, explains possible harm, and recommends a justified responsible action.","explanation":"A structured analysis first explains the event and who is affected. It then identifies relevant ethical areas and the possible harms before recommending a justified response. The goal is reasoned responsibility, not simply","example":"labelling an online action good or bad. Suppose an organisation reposts a customer image without permission. An analysis should explain who is affected, consider authorship and privacy, identify possible harms and recommend a response that can be justified.","full":"A method that describes what happened, identifies who is affected, selects relevant ethical areas, explains possible harm, and recommends a justified responsible action.\n\nA structured analysis first explains the event and who is affected. It then identifies relevant ethical areas and the possible harms before recommending a justified response. The goal is reasoned responsibility, not simply\n\nlabelling an online action good or bad.\n\nSuppose an organisation reposts a customer image without permission. An analysis should explain who is affected, consider authorship and privacy, identify possible harms and recommend a response that can be justified.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":133,"term":"Context collapse","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":37,"definition":"When different audiences and contexts converge in one place, so something meant for one group is seen and read by others.","explanation":"An online post can reach audiences who would normally be separate, such as friends, teachers and employers. A message intended for one setting may be interpreted differently in another. The effect is increased risk of misunderstanding because the original social context is missing.","example":"A joke posted for friends might later be seen by colleagues or strangers who lack the original context. The same words can be interpreted differently when different audiences meet in one space.","full":"When different audiences and contexts converge in one place, so something meant for one group is seen and read by others.\n\nAn online post can reach audiences who would normally be separate, such as friends, teachers and employers. A message intended for one setting may be interpreted differently in another. The effect is increased risk of misunderstanding because the original social context is missing.\n\nA joke posted for friends might later be seen by colleagues or strangers who lack the original context. The same words can be interpreted differently when different audiences meet in one space.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":134,"term":"Online disinhibition effect","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":37,"definition":"People say and do things online that they would not ordinarily say and do face to face (Suler, 2004).","explanation":"People may behave differently when communicating online than they would face to face. The lecture cites Suler’s explanation of how online settings can change what individuals feel comfortable saying or doing. The concept is relevant to evaluating responsibility in digital interactions.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"People say and do things online that they would not ordinarily say and do face to face (Suler, 2004).\n\nPeople may behave differently when communicating online than they would face to face. The lecture cites Suler’s explanation of how online settings can change what individuals feel comfortable saying or doing. The concept is relevant to evaluating responsibility in digital interactions.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":135,"term":"First-party data","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":37,"definition":"Data a company collects directly from its own audience, such as customers or website visitors.","explanation":"The company collects this data through its own direct relationships with visitors or customers. Examples in the course include information from its audience and website visitors. Its source is different from information aggregated by an outside data broker.","example":"For example, information collected through a company’s own website or customer relationship is first-party data. Its direct source distinguishes it from data purchased from a provider that collected it elsewhere.","full":"Data a company collects directly from its own audience, such as customers or website visitors.\n\nThe company collects this data through its own direct relationships with visitors or customers. Examples in the course include information from its audience and website visitors. Its source is different from information aggregated by an outside data broker.\n\nFor example, information collected through a company’s own website or customer relationship is first-party data. Its direct source distinguishes it from data purchased from a provider that collected it elsewhere.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":136,"term":"Third-party data","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":37,"definition":"Data aggregated and sold by providers who did not collect it themselves.","explanation":"The data comes from providers that aggregate and sell information they did not collect directly from the people concerned. The course distinguishes it from first-party data because the organisation buying it is further removed from the original collection relationship. That distinction matters when considering transparency and privacy.","example":"A business obtaining an audience dataset from a separate data provider is using third-party data. Unlike first-party data, the buyer did not collect it directly through its own customer relationship.","full":"Data aggregated and sold by providers who did not collect it themselves.\n\nThe data comes from providers that aggregate and sell information they did not collect directly from the people concerned. The course distinguishes it from first-party data because the organisation buying it is further removed from the original collection relationship. That distinction matters when considering transparency and privacy.\n\nA business obtaining an audience dataset from a separate data provider is using third-party data. Unlike first-party data, the buyer did not collect it directly through its own customer relationship.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":137,"term":"Second-party data","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":38,"definition":"Another company's first-party data, shared through a direct partnership.","explanation":"One organisation shares its directly collected first-party data with another through a partnership. From the recipient’s perspective it is second-party data. The term therefore describes the relationship between the original collector and the partner using it.","example":"If two organisations form a partnership and one shares data it collected directly, that information is second-party data for the recipient. The label depends on the direct partnership.","full":"Another company's first-party data, shared through a direct partnership.\n\nOne organisation shares its directly collected first-party data with another through a partnership. From the recipient’s perspective it is second-party data. The term therefore describes the relationship between the original collector and the partner using it.\n\nIf two organisations form a partnership and one shares data it collected directly, that information is second-party data for the recipient. The label depends on the direct partnership.","category":"ETHICS ONLINE"},{"id":138,"term":"Persistence","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":38,"definition":"The quality of online content that allows it to remain accessible, or to survive through copies, long after it was published.","explanation":"Online information may remain available long after its original posting. Copies can preserve material even when the first version is removed. This increases the long-term consequences of publishing and sharing.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The quality of online content that allows it to remain accessible, or to survive through copies, long after it was published.\n\nOnline information may remain available long after its original posting. Copies can preserve material even when the first version is removed. This increases the long-term consequences of publishing and sharing.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE CONTEXT"},{"id":139,"term":"Searchability (online context)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":38,"definition":"The quality of online content that allows it to be found by people you did not intend.","explanation":"Online information can be discovered through searches by people outside the intended audience. A post written for a small group may later become easy for strangers to find. The course treats this as one of the qualities that changes the social consequences of communication.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The quality of online content that allows it to be found by people you did not intend.\n\nOnline information can be discovered through searches by people outside the intended audience. A post written for a small group may later become easy for strangers to find. The course treats this as one of the qualities that changes the social consequences of communication.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE CONTEXT"},{"id":140,"term":"Scalability (online context)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":38,"definition":"The quality of online content that allows it to reach far larger audiences than intended.","explanation":"A message can travel far beyond the small audience its author imagined. Online networks can extend its reach quickly and widely. The consequence is that a private-feeling interaction may have public-scale effects.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The quality of online content that allows it to reach far larger audiences than intended.\n\nA message can travel far beyond the small audience its author imagined. Online networks can extend its reach quickly and widely. The consequence is that a private-feeling interaction may have public-scale effects.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE CONTEXT"},{"id":141,"term":"Replicability (online context)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":38,"definition":"The quality of online content that allows it to be copied and reused in other contexts.","explanation":"Digital messages can be copied, reproduced and redistributed with little connection to their original setting. The recipient may encounter only a fragment or screenshot. Because copies persist elsewhere, the original author cannot fully control later uses.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The quality of online content that allows it to be copied and reused in other contexts.\n\nDigital messages can be copied, reproduced and redistributed with little connection to their original setting. The recipient may encounter only a fragment or screenshot. Because copies persist elsewhere, the original author cannot fully control later uses.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE CONTEXT"},{"id":142,"term":"Thumbocracy","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":39,"definition":"Serneberg's term for an affirmation-seeking online culture shaped by the thumb and visible approval signals such as likes.","explanation":"The lecturer uses the term for online participation shaped by the thumb and visible signals of approval. Likes and similar interactions can encourage people to seek affirmation through countable reactions. It connects interface design to identity and social behaviour.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Serneberg's term for an affirmation-seeking online culture shaped by the thumb and visible approval signals such as likes.\n\nThe lecturer uses the term for online participation shaped by the thumb and visible signals of approval. Likes and similar interactions can encourage people to seek affirmation through countable reactions. It connects interface design to identity and social behaviour.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"IDENTITY"},{"id":143,"term":"Identity (ethical area)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":39,"definition":"How people present themselves and manage their digital personas online.","explanation":"Identity concerns how individuals construct and present themselves in digital settings. People may manage profiles or personas differently depending on who they expect to see them. Ethical problems can arise when these identities are misrepresented or exposed to unintended audiences.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"How people present themselves and manage their digital personas online.\n\nIdentity concerns how individuals construct and present themselves in digital settings. People may manage profiles or personas differently depending on who they expect to see them. Ethical problems can arise when these identities are misrepresented or exposed to unintended audiences.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICAL FAULT LINES"},{"id":144,"term":"Privacy (ethical area)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":39,"definition":"Decisions about sharing personal information and respecting the privacy of other people.","explanation":"Privacy concerns choices about personal information, including one’s own data and other people’s information. The ethical question is not only whether data can be shared, but whether doing so respects the people affected. It therefore extends beyond the convenience of collecting or publishing information.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Decisions about sharing personal information and respecting the privacy of other people.\n\nPrivacy concerns choices about personal information, including one’s own data and other people’s information. The ethical question is not only whether data can be shared, but whether doing so respects the people affected. It therefore extends beyond the convenience of collecting or publishing information.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICAL FAULT LINES"},{"id":145,"term":"Ownership and authorship","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":39,"definition":"The ethical area concerned with who owns digital content and who receives credit for it.","explanation":"This area asks who owns digital material and who should receive credit for creating it. Content that is easy to copy online still has an author or origin. Ethical participation requires considering permission and attribution rather than assuming access means unrestricted use.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The ethical area concerned with who owns digital content and who receives credit for it.\n\nThis area asks who owns digital material and who should receive credit for creating it. Content that is easy to copy online still has an author or origin. Ethical participation requires considering permission and attribution rather than assuming access means unrestricted use.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICAL FAULT LINES"},{"id":146,"term":"Credibility (ethical area)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":40,"definition":"The ethical area concerned with one's own trustworthiness online and the assessment of other people and sources.","explanation":"Credibility concerns both being trustworthy oneself and judging whether online material from others deserves trust. A post, claim or source can spread widely without being reliable. Ethical online behaviour includes examining what supports information before passing it on.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The ethical area concerned with one's own trustworthiness online and the assessment of other people and sources.\n\nCredibility concerns both being trustworthy oneself and judging whether online material from others deserves trust. A post, claim or source can spread widely without being reliable. Ethical online behaviour includes examining what supports information before passing it on.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICAL FAULT LINES"},{"id":147,"term":"Participation (ethical area)","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":40,"definition":"The ethical area concerned with conduct, social relations and responsibilities in online communities.","explanation":"Participation addresses how people treat one another in online groups and communities. It includes the responsibilities that come with commenting, sharing and interacting. The course connects meaningful participation with considering the effect of one’s conduct on others.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"The ethical area concerned with conduct, social relations and responsibilities in online communities.\n\nParticipation addresses how people treat one another in online groups and communities. It includes the responsibilities that come with commenting, sharing and interacting. The course connects meaningful participation with considering the effect of one’s conduct on others.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ETHICAL FAULT LINES"},{"id":148,"term":"Explicit data","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":40,"definition":"Information a person knowingly provides, such as profile details and posts.","explanation":"A person directly supplies explicit data when they fill out a profile or post information. The important feature is that the information is knowingly provided. The course contrasts this with implicit data inferred from actions and behaviour.","example":"For example, information that a person types into a profile field is explicitly provided. It differs from assumptions generated by analysing the person’s behaviour.","full":"Information a person knowingly provides, such as profile details and posts.\n\nA person directly supplies explicit data when they fill out a profile or post information. The important feature is that the information is knowingly provided. The course contrasts this with implicit data inferred from actions and behaviour.\n\nFor example, information that a person types into a profile field is explicitly provided. It differs from assumptions generated by analysing the person’s behaviour.","category":"PRIVACY AND DATA"},{"id":149,"term":"Implicit data","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":40,"definition":"Information inferred from behaviour, such as clicks, time spent and location.","explanation":"Implicit data is drawn from patterns of behaviour rather than necessarily being typed into a form. Clicks, time spent and location can be used to infer information about a user. The distinction raises questions about whether people understand what their behaviour reveals.","example":"A platform can observe which content a person clicks or how long they spend on it, and infer preferences from those actions. The user has not necessarily stated those preferences directly.","full":"Information inferred from behaviour, such as clicks, time spent and location.\n\nImplicit data is drawn from patterns of behaviour rather than necessarily being typed into a form. Clicks, time spent and location can be used to infer information about a user. The distinction raises questions about whether people understand what their behaviour reveals.\n\nA platform can observe which content a person clicks or how long they spend on it, and infer preferences from those actions. The user has not necessarily stated those preferences directly.","category":"PRIVACY AND DATA"},{"id":150,"term":"FOMO","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":40,"definition":"Fear of missing out.","explanation":"Fear of missing out describes anxiety about being absent from experiences or activities others may be having. Digital feeds can make these alternatives visible and constantly updated. The term helps explain some","example":"motivations for repeated online checking. The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Fear of missing out.\n\nFear of missing out describes anxiety about being absent from experiences or activities others may be having. Digital feeds can make these alternatives visible and constantly updated. The term helps explain some\n\nmotivations for repeated online checking.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE BEHAVIOUR"},{"id":151,"term":"FOBO","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":41,"definition":"Fear of better options.","explanation":"Fear of better options describes hesitation caused by the possibility that a better alternative exists. Having many choices available can make committing to one feel difficult. The concept captures a different pressure from FOMO: uncertainty about choosing rather than exclusion from an experience.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"Fear of better options.\n\nFear of better options describes hesitation caused by the possibility that a better alternative exists. Having many choices available can make committing to one feel difficult. The concept captures a different pressure from FOMO: uncertainty about choosing rather than exclusion from an experience.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE BEHAVIOUR"},{"id":152,"term":"IWWIWWIWI","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":41,"definition":"I want what I want when I want it.","explanation":"The phrase expresses an expectation of immediate access and satisfaction: “I want what I want when I want it.” The lecture uses it to describe a culture of on-demand digital experiences. Users become accustomed to choosing the content or service at a time that suits them.","example":"The lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","full":"I want what I want when I want it.\n\nThe phrase expresses an expectation of immediate access and satisfaction: “I want what I want when I want it.” The lecture uses it to describe a culture of on-demand digital experiences. Users become accustomed to choosing the content or service at a time that suits them.\n\nThe lecture uses this concept to question what people experience online and what responsibilities arise when digital systems shape visibility, information or interaction.","category":"ONLINE BEHAVIOUR"},{"id":153,"term":"Appointment culture","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":41,"definition":"A media culture in which audiences watch at the same scheduled broadcast time.","explanation":"In appointment culture, media consumption follows a set schedule and people must watch at the designated broadcast time. Audiences are organised around the provider’s timetable. The course contrasts this with the greater freedom and participation of engagement culture.","example":"A scheduled television programme requires viewers to tune in at a particular time. The timetable is determined by the broadcaster rather than chosen separately by each viewer.","full":"A media culture in which audiences watch at the same scheduled broadcast time.\n\nIn appointment culture, media consumption follows a set schedule and people must watch at the designated broadcast time. Audiences are organised around the provider’s timetable. The course contrasts this with the greater freedom and participation of engagement culture.\n\nA scheduled television programme requires viewers to tune in at a particular time. The timetable is determined by the broadcaster rather than chosen separately by each viewer.","category":"PARTICIPATION"},{"id":154,"term":"Engagement culture","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":41,"definition":"A media culture in which audiences watch, discuss and participate on their own terms.","explanation":"Audiences can watch, discuss, share and participate when and how they choose. This changes the relationship between media publishers and audiences, who are no longer only scheduled viewers. It also connects consumption with participation and social interaction.","example":"A viewer can watch on demand, discuss content with others and join conversations outside a fixed broadcast time. This adds participation to consumption.","full":"A media culture in which audiences watch, discuss and participate on their own terms.\n\nAudiences can watch, discuss, share and participate when and how they choose. This changes the relationship between media publishers and audiences, who are no longer only scheduled viewers. It also connects consumption with participation and social interaction.\n\nA viewer can watch on demand, discuss content with others and join conversations outside a fixed broadcast time. This adds participation to consumption.","category":"PARTICIPATION"},{"id":155,"term":"Binge-watching","lecture":"Lecture 8 · The advertising marketplace and online ethics","page":42,"definition":"Watching multiple episodes, or an entire season, of a programme in one sitting.","explanation":"Binge-watching occurs when someone watches several episodes in succession instead of waiting for separate scheduled broadcasts. It is a clear example of on-demand habits. The concept connects with the shift from appointment culture toward audiences choosing their own viewing patterns.","example":"Watching several episodes consecutively rather than waiting for the next scheduled broadcast illustrates the on-demand habits described in the lecture.","full":"Watching multiple episodes, or an entire season, of a programme in one sitting.\n\nBinge-watching occurs when someone watches several episodes in succession instead of waiting for separate scheduled broadcasts. It is a clear example of on-demand habits. The concept connects with the shift from appointment culture toward audiences choosing their own viewing patterns.\n\nWatching several episodes consecutively rather than waiting for the next scheduled broadcast illustrates the on-demand habits described in the lecture."}];